{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"I’m using the repository’s code-review checklist and will trace the revised three-phase edit closure against the current transactor, index, schema, and tests. I’ll pay particular attention to the deleted snapshot abstraction, the undetectable miswiring risk, and whether every removed test obligation has an explicit durable discharge."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md; sed -n '1,260p' docs/design/datom.world.md; nl -ba collab/1788948293243-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '1,300p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md; sed -n '1,260p' docs/design/datom.world.md; nl -ba collab/1788948293243-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '1,300p'\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: code-review-pr\ndescription: >-\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\n---\n\n# Code Review PR Skill\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n  - problematic code\n  + suggested fix\n  ```\n\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\n\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\n\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Utility Script Reference\n\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\n\n---\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls\n\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, fundamental primitives (tuples, streams), design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams. Persistent facts are immutable tuples and datoms `[e a v t m]`.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is events on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata).\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly. Streams carry whatever values the consumer needs.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in Yin.VM that communicate with the outside world through DaoStream. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/agent.tzu.md`](./agent.tzu.md) and [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for agent and VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nHost-specific boundaries are isolated with DaoStream and interpreters of\nDaoStream. A host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call.\n\nDaoStream handle operations are the one sanctioned function-shaped contact\nwith that boundary: their exhaustive outcome maps are stream protocol, not an\napplication callback. Above those operations, portable code still expresses\nhost work as effect values and observes outcomes as stream data.\n\nPortable code appends an effect value describing what must happen. A host\ninterpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and appends the\noutcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by identity in the\ndata; neither side holds a reference to the other.\n\nAn adapter that exposes a function for portable code to call is not an\ninterpreter. It isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the\neffect never appears as an emission.\n\nA callback is an event on a stream. That is the same rule in the other\ndirection: when the host initiates -- a socket receives, a connection drops, a\ntimer fires -- the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\n\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**An adapter is a map of those functions. That is all an adapter is.** It is a\nvalue, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files, timers and windows\nall deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is what they call. The\nhost library is handed the map. Each entry takes the host's arguments and\nreturns one value; the append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry\ncannot return a value to the host and cannot invoke application code. The\nadapter is handed only the deposit operation and its own host resource. If its\ndeposit channel fails, it may close that resource to make the boundary\nobservably gone; this is teardown, not an upward callback. Host types are\nclassified into plain data here -- a transform is the last place holding a host\nerror object, and no host type may cross onto the stream.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read, and it does\nnot need to. This is what keeps the layering one-way: a boundary depends on\nnothing above it, because whoever constructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of those events semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol\nvalidation happen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport\nmachinery, not an event the adapter is obliged to deposit. Once the transport\nhas produced a valid host event, data is syntax (axiom 2), so worth is not a\nproperty of the datum -- an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to\nanother that does not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys\nthe perspectives that have not been taken. How much is retained is the medium's\nretention policy, which is operational; what an event means is interpretation.\nNeither is the depositor's decision.\n\nAn adapter that takes a function to invoke has relocated the callback, not\nremoved it, even when every value it passes is plain data. An adapter whose\ncallback has a meaningful return value is worse: that is a synchronous call in\nboth directions wearing the shape of an event.\n\nHost events are ordinary values on an ordinary stream. A transport may use a\nqualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and additive evolution;\nthat envelope remains plain data and creates no separate event mechanism. A\ncomposition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary for facts that it\nowns. Neither requires a bespoke bus or callback API.\n\nHost error types never cross the boundary. A transform function classifies them\nand emits a qualified value.\n\nA host with no implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported\nresult. That is a correct outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\nFor advanced topics (parallel transport, capability tokens, entanglement):\nSee [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md)\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/website.md`](../agents/website.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-09 11:12 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-09 18:12 +0700 (Asia/Bangkok)\n     3\tCoding-Agent: claude (Opus 5)\n     4\tSession-ID: 6378e1b0-1c5d-4e10-8ea0-61551c03c828\n     5\t\n     6\t# Plan: dao.space.transactor and dao.space.index → dao.stream (r3)\n     7\t\n     8\t## Context\n     9\t\n    10\t`dao.space.transactor` is the seam between v1 and v2: it validates its intake\n    11\tpool with v2 `stream/writer?` but requires its local stream to satisfy the v1\n    12\treader and writer protocols, and it is itself a v1 stream registered through\n    13\t`ds/defopen`. The index plan's D1 established that index cannot close until\n    14\tthis namespace moves, because `publish-index!`'s input *is* the transactor's\n    15\tlocal stream. This sweep ends with **both** `dao.space.transactor` and\n    16\t`dao.space.index` free of `dao.stream`, and `dao.space.schema` the only\n    17\t`dao.space` namespace still on v1, under its own plan.\n    18\t\n    19\tMethod: an explicit invariants list is the contract, marked `[D]` stated in a\n    20\tdesign, `[T]` pinned only by a test, `[T→D]` test-pinned and worth promoting,\n    21\t`[T✗]` an implementation accident dropped with its reason, and — new in r3 —\n    22\t`[D✗]` a *stated* invariant retired because the transport now discharges it\n    23\tstructurally. The existing implementation and tests have no authority beyond\n    24\tthe invariants they pin. Old code is deleted in the phase that replaces it.\n    25\tThis plan is deleted when consumed, so it must leave nothing owed.\n    26\t\n    27\t### What r3 changes\n    28\t\n    29\tr2 was reviewed and did not survive. The P0 is accepted in full: `:oldest` is\n    30\tthe earliest *retained* position, so `derive-next-t` would have derived\n    31\ttransaction time from a truncated suffix, silently, on a tree with every lane\n    32\tgreen. r2's answer to it — a capacity constant and a\n    33\t`retained-history-survives-reopen-at-capacity` test — was worse than the\n    34\tdefect: that test cannot produce the gap it asserts (a fresh `:oldest` after\n    35\teviction reports nothing), so it would have pinned a defect as a feature.\n    36\t\n    37\t`dao.stream.memory-log` (`ba90b3a`) and three contract commits\n    38\t(`2e25b5c`, `ed30d7b`, `ff44107`) replace all of it. Concretely in this plan:\n    39\t\n    40\t| r2 | r3 |\n    41\t|---|---|\n    42\t| §6, the capacity hazard, 26–29 ring-buffer sites, one capacity constant, `retained-history-survives-reopen-at-capacity` | **deleted entirely.** The sites are `dao.space` tests opening a local stream; they open a `memory-log`. There is no number to pick — the transport has no capacity key, and passing one is `invalid-spec`. |\n    43\t| Phase 2, promoting `query/snapshot` to `observe/snapshot` | **deleted.** See D5. |\n    44\t| \"read from position zero\" as the completeness invariant | restated on the transport's declared retention (D5, S1, S9) |\n    45\t| Phase 4 (docs) and Phase 5 (index close), claimed order-independent | **false, per review P2.** Merged: each code phase carries its own doc edits, so every phase ends with truthful documentation. |\n    46\t| five phases | **three.** Phase 3 here is r2's Phase 5 — the index close — kept in full. |\n    47\t\n    48\tThe six findings of `1788874517712-review-space-transactor-index-plan` are\n    49\tauthoritative where they differ from r2. Each is addressed at the point it\n    50\tbites, and section 10 indexes them.\n    51\t\n    52\t---\n    53\t\n    54\t## 1. Decisions\n    55\t\n    56\t### D1 — a transactor is not a stream in v2. It is an interpreter over one.\n    57\t\n    58\t`dao.stream.md`'s *Composition* already names this case: \"anything that\n    59\ttransforms one stream into another is an interpreter — it reads via its own\n    60\tcursor and appends to an ordinary output stream — and that lives entirely\n    61\toutside this contract, needing no support from it.\" Take the three v1 surfaces\n    62\tone at a time:\n    63\t\n    64\t- **`append!`** is not a stream append. It takes an entity map or a datom\n    65\t  vector, allocates transaction time, and writes a *transaction record*; it\n    66\t  answers `{:result :ok :t t :datoms datoms}`, a transaction receipt, not a\n    67\t  write outcome. Making that conform to `IDaoStreamWriter` would mean either\n    68\t  lying about the value domain or losing the receipt.\n    69\t- **`next`** (:164) is pure delegation: `(ds/next local-stream cursor)`. The\n    70\t  caller *supplied* the local stream and still holds it, so the delegate adds\n    71\t  nothing but a second name for one sequence. Nothing outside `transactor_test`\n    72\t  calls it — confirmed by the review.\n    73\t- **`close!`/`closed?`** close nothing. They are a per-handle write gate over\n    74\t  state the wrapper owns.\n    75\t\n    76\tSo the transactor becomes a **plain value with explicit named operations**, the\n    77\tsame move `dao.space.query.md`'s *Bounded realizations are values, not\n    78\tstreams* made for the relation transport and `query/open-published!` made for\n    79\tthe opened index:\n    80\t\n    81\t```clojure\n    82\t(def local (:dao.stream/handle\n    83\t             (memory-log/create! {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/memory-log})))\n    84\t\n    85\t(def log (transactor/create! {:local-stream local\n    86\t                              :intake-pool [intake]      ; v2 writers\n    87\t                              :name \"worker-7\"}))        ; optional\n    88\t\n    89\t(transactor/append!   log {:db/id 1 :work/claims \"task\"})\n    90\t(transactor/transact! log [{:db/id 1 :work/claims \"task\"} …])\n    91\t(transactor/publish!  log opts)\n    92\t(transactor/close!    log)\n    93\t;; reads: use `local` — it is the caller's handle and always was\n    94\t```\n    95\t\n    96\t`next` is dropped (T12). `closed?` is dropped (T14): v2 lists a `closed?`\n    97\tpredicate as *Explicitly Absent* because \"a predicate answer is stale the\n    98\tmoment it returns; operation results are authoritative,\" and the replacement\n    99\tis already in the vocabulary — `append!`/`transact!` on a closed transactor\n   100\tanswer `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}` as data.\n   101\t\n   102\t### D2 — `ds/defopen :transactor` is replaced by a plain constructor, and the descriptor was never portable.\n   103\t\n   104\tv2 has no registry (*Explicitly Absent*: \"an ambient registry — a\n   105\tnamespace-global dispatch table, or registration as a load-time side effect\").\n   106\tThe replacement is `transactor/create!`, a plain function the caller calls,\n   107\tfollowing `query/open-published!`. The owner did not overrule the name.\n   108\t\n   109\t**The descriptor was never a descriptor.** v2's `descriptor` operation is\n   110\t*reachability* data — `valid-descriptor?` requires a portable envelope, and\n   111\tthe whole point is naming a stream across a serialization boundary. The\n   112\t`:transactor` map holds two live handles under `:local-stream` and\n   113\t`:intake-pool`; it could never cross that boundary, so it was a v1 *dispatch*\n   114\trecord wearing the word \"descriptor\". It becomes the constructor's plain\n   115\toptions map. Call it a **spec**, not a descriptor, so the reachability word is\n   116\tnot imported into something that has none. A transactor implements no v2\n   117\tprotocol at all, `IDaoStreamDescriptor` included: it is not on a stream, so it\n   118\thas no identity to project.\n   119\t\n   120\t`create!` validates **surfaces, never retention**. `stream/reader?` and\n   121\t`stream/writer?` are v2's own public gating functions and stay; retention is\n   122\t\"declared, never interrogated,\" and `dao.stream.md`'s *Explicitly Absent* now\n   123\tlists a retention predicate for the same reason `closed?` is absent. A\n   124\treviewer should not read the surface checks as licence for a retention check:\n   125\tthe two are different questions and the contract answers them differently.\n   126\t\n   127\tReturn shape: **the value, or a throw** — not an outcome map. `create!`'s\n   128\tfailure modes are a malformed spec and a malformed retained history, both of\n   129\twhich the caller can only abort on. This keeps `create!` aligned with\n   130\t`index/publish-index!` and `query/open-published!`.\n   131\t\n   132\t### D3 — operational outcomes become data; argument defects keep throwing.\n   133\t\n   134\t`append-packet!` (:110-122) throws when the local append answers anything but\n   135\t`{:result :ok}`. In v2 the local append answers `ok | full | invalid-value |\n   136\tclosed | transport-error`, and **`full` means \"not yet, retry\"** — throwing on\n   137\tit was always wrong, because it converts a normal backpressure signal into an\n   138\texception the caller must catch to obey the retry contract the docstring\n   139\talready promises.\n   140\t\n   141\t| Case | v1 | v2 |\n   142\t|---|---|---|\n   143\t| local append ok | `{:result :ok :t t :datoms ds}`, watermark advances | `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok :dao.space/t t :dao.space/datoms ds}`, watermark advances |\n   144\t| local append `full`/`closed`/`invalid-value`/`transport-error` | throws | returned as data, watermark **unchanged**, same `t` still pending |\n   145\t| local append answers a non-outcome | throws | folded to `:dao.stream/transport-error` with `:dao.stream/answer` retained |\n   146\t| transactor itself closed | throws | `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}` |\n   147\t| malformed entity map / explicit datom `t` / empty `tx-data` / zero datoms | throws | **still throws** |\n   148\t| local append throws | propagates, watermark unchanged | unchanged |\n   149\t\n   150\tThe last row of throws stays because those are defects in the caller's own\n   151\targument, detected before any stream is touched — the transactor's contract\n   152\twith its caller, not the stream's outcome algebra.\n   153\t\n   154\tWorth noting rather than hiding: on a `memory-log` local stream, the only\n   155\treachable non-ok outcome is `closed`, since the transport excludes `full`,\n   156\t`invalid-value` and `transport-error`. The branch is not dead code for an\n   157\texcluded outcome — `append-packet!` is a library function over whatever handle\n   158\tit is given, and its `case` must be total. But no test can induce `full` on\n   159\tthe real transport, which is why P1's schema fix below is tested with a\n   160\t**writer double** rather than by trying to fill a log that cannot fill.\n   161\t\n   162\t**The retry contract gets stronger, not weaker.** It was prose plus a throw; it\n   163\tbecomes checkable: *the watermark advances if and only if the local append\n   164\tanswered `:dao.stream/ok`*, so retrying the identical call re-attempts the\n   165\tidentical `t`.\n   166\t\n   167\tValidate the local stream's answer with `stream/valid-outcome?` before\n   168\tbranching, as `observe/valid-or-transport-error` does. That helper is private\n   169\tto `observe`; a four-line local copy is right rather than widening `observe`'s\n   170\tsurface for one caller — note the duplication so a third copy triggers\n   171\tpromotion instead of accumulating.\n   172\t\n   173\t### D4 — `derive-next-t` keeps its O(history) replay, and is now actually correct.\n   174\t\n   175\tKeep, unchanged, and record the cost rather than paying for it here.\n   176\t\n   177\tThe local stream is the **only durable truth**. The watermark is derived state,\n   178\tso it must be recoverable from that truth; any stored counter is a second\n   179\tdurable truth that can disagree with the first, and reconciling two truths is a\n   180\tdesign, not an optimization.\n   181\t\n   182\tWhat changed is the ground it stands on. Under r2, `derive-next-t` was not\n   183\tmerely expensive but *wrong*: it replayed whatever suffix survived. It is\n   184\tcorrect now **because the transport cannot evict**, not because it mints\n   185\t`:oldest`. The scan remains the same walk `publish-index!` performs, and\n   186\t`dao.space.index.md`'s \"full retention is required\" stops being a hope and\n   187\tbecomes the wiring requirement of S9.\n   188\t\n   189\tNote in *Open items* that the eventual relief keeps one truth — the log carries\n   190\tits own checkpoint — rather than adding a second. Do not design it here.\n   191\t\n   192\t### D5 — no `observe/snapshot` promotion, no `observe/drain`, no shared retention law.\n   193\t\n   194\tr2 promoted `query/snapshot`'s loop into `dao.stream.observe` on the\n   195\tgrounds that index's snapshot would be a second consumer. **Delete that\n   196\tphase.** The transport changed the argument:\n   197\t\n   198\t- `query/snapshot` exists to classify *every* stopping outcome as data because\n   199\t  its input's nature is unknown — it takes any v2 reader handle a caller has.\n   200\t- `index/snapshot-datoms` reads a handle whose nature is **declared**: complete\n   201\t  retention, `gap` excluded, `transport-error` excluded. Three outcomes are\n   202\t  reachable: `ok`, `blocked`, `end`.\n   203\t\n   204\tSharing one loop would make index carry query's outcome vocabulary for\n   205\tconditions its transport excludes, which is exactly what `conformance.cljc`'s\n   206\texclusion principle warns against: an exclusion is discharged by a structural\n   207\targument about the transport, and handling code for an excluded outcome is\n   208\tuntested code that merely *looks* like a safety net. Index's read is ten lines\n   209\twith three branches; it is not a loop worth sharing yet.\n   210\t\n   211\tRecording the rule for whoever revisits it: there is **no `observe/drain`** —\n   212\tthe name imports the destructive-read vocabulary the contract removed into the\n   213\tnamespace named for observation — and if the mechanism is ever shared it keeps\n   214\tthe name `snapshot`. A generic complete-retention law in the conformance\n   215\tharness is likewise deferred until a second complete-history transport exists;\n   216\tone transport is not a generalization.\n   217\t\n   218\t### D6 — what `snapshot-datoms` becomes, and which throw survives\n   219\t\n   220\t```clojure\n   221\t(defn snapshot-datoms\n   222\t  \"Read an agent-local stream in full and flatten it to canonical local d5\n   223\t   datoms.\n   224\t\n   225\t   The local stream is on a complete-retention transport\n   226\t   (dao.stream.memory-log), so a fresh :oldest cursor is the origin and\n   227\t   `gap` cannot occur — completeness comes from the transport's declared\n   228\t   retention, never from the anchor (dao.stream.md, *Complete history*).\n   229\t   `blocked` (an open stream caught up) and `end` (a closed one fully read)\n   230\t   finish the read at the tail. Any other outcome means the handle is not the\n   231\t   transport the composition owes; malformed elements throw through\n   232\t   datoms-from-elements.\"\n   233\t  [local-stream]\n   234\t  (let [mint (stream/cursor local-stream :dao.stream/oldest)]\n   235\t    (when-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome mint))\n   236\t      (throw (ex-info \"local stream refused an :oldest cursor\" {:result mint})))\n   237\t    (loop [cursor (:dao.stream/cursor mint)\n   238\t           elements []]\n   239\t      (let [r (stream/next local-stream cursor)]\n   240\t        (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n   241\t          :dao.stream/ok (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r)\n   242\t                                (conj elements (:dao.stream/value r)))\n   243\t          (:dao.stream/blocked :dao.stream/end) (datoms-from-elements elements)\n   244\t          (throw (ex-info \"local stream is not a complete-retention transport\"\n   245\t                          {:outcome (:dao.stream/outcome r) :result r})))))))\n   246\t```\n   247\t\n   248\t**The gap branch is deleted; one totality branch survives.** The owner asked\n   249\tfor the call and the reasoning, so both:\n   250\t\n   251\tThe gap throw bought nothing even before the transport existed. On a ring\n   252\tbuffer a fresh `:oldest` never gaps — that is the whole P0 — so the branch\n   253\tcould not have caught the failure it appeared to guard. Keeping it now would\n   254\tbe handling code for a structurally excluded outcome, and would additionally\n   255\timply that index tolerates evicting transports, which is the wiring defect the\n   256\tcontract now names.\n   257\t\n   258\tWhat survives is not defensive code for an excluded outcome but the `case`'s\n   259\town totality, which the outcome algebra requires: a *conforming* transport can\n   260\tanswer `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor`, `transport-error` or `gap`, and\n   261\tthis function is a library function over whatever handle it is given. Throwing\n   262\tis index's policy above a total read, the same layering as everywhere else.\n   263\t\n   264\tAnd the honest limit, stated rather than papered over: **this branch cannot\n   265\tcatch a wrongly wired ring buffer.** A ring buffer answers `ok`/`blocked`/`end`\n   266\tfrom a fresh `:oldest` just as the memory-log does. Nothing inside index can\n   267\tdetect the defect, by the contract's own design — there is no retention\n   268\tpredicate and there must not be one. The protection is the declaration in S9\n   269\tand the composition that honours it, not a runtime branch. Any future reviewer\n   270\ttempted to add a check here should read `dao.stream.md`'s *Explicitly Absent*\n   271\tfirst.\n   272\t\n   273\t### D7 — index ends this sweep requiring no `dao.stream`\n   274\t\n   275\tVerified against the tree. Every `ds/` occurrence in\n   276\t`src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc`:\n   277\t\n   278\t| line | use | closed by |\n   279\t|---|---|---|\n   280\t| :347 | `ds/IDaoStreamReader` (PublishedIndexStream impl) | Phase 3 |\n   281\t| :358 | `ds/IDaoStreamBound` (PublishedIndexStream impl) | Phase 3 |\n   282\t| :383 | `ds/defopen :dao.space.index/published` | Phase 3 |\n   283\t| :444 | the string \"ds/next\" in `snapshot-datoms`' docstring | Phase 2 |\n   284\t| :453 | `(ds/next local-stream cursor)` | Phase 2 |\n   285\t\n   286\tFive occurrences, one of them prose. There is no\n   287\t`#?(:cljs (:require-macros [dao.stream]))` in `index.cljc`; its `ns` form ends\n   288\tat :41.\n   289\t\n   290\t**At the end of Phase 3, `dao.space.index` requires exactly**\n   291\t`dao.data.btree`, `dao.data.btree.storage`, `dao.datom`, `dao.jing`, and\n   292\t`dao.stream`. Two removals beyond `dao.stream` follow and must be\n   293\tconfirmed, not assumed: `dao.jing.coordinate` is used only by the deleted\n   294\t`defopen`, and `dao.stream.observe` is **never added**, because D5 keeps\n   295\tindex off `observe`. The check is\n   296\t`grep -n \"jing-coordinate/\\|ds/\\|observe/\" src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc`\n   297\treturning nothing.\n   298\t\n   299\t### D8 — schema moves at its call sites only, and the two review defects there are fixed\n   300\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1788948293243-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '299,610p'; nl -ba collab/1788948293243-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '611,860p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1788948293243-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '299,610p'; nl -ba collab/1788948293243-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '611,860p'\"","aggregated_output":"   299\t### D8 — schema moves at its call sites only, and the two review defects there are fixed\n   300\t\n   301\t`SchemaWrapper` implements only `ds/IDaoStreamBound`, and both methods delegate\n   302\tto the transactor handle. When the transactor drops `closed?`, schema holds\n   303\tthe flag itself — it already has a per-wrapper `state` atom for exactly this\n   304\tkind of coordination.\n   305\t\n   306\tWhat Phase 2 forces in schema, and nothing more:\n   307\t\n   308\t| schema site | forced edit |\n   309\t|---|---|\n   310\t| `:965` `ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :transactor …}` | → `tx/create!` |\n   311\t| `:949` `close!` → `(ds/close! inner)` | set own `:closed`, call `tx/close!`, and **return `{:woke []}`** — review P1(4) |\n   312\t| `:954` `closed?` → `(ds/closed? inner)` | read own `:closed` |\n   313\t| `:1112-1114` `(tx/transact! …)` then unconditional `(reset! lock next-state)` | **install `next-state` only on `:dao.stream/ok`** — review P1(2) |\n   314\t| `:1127` `schema/publish!`'s unlocked `ds/closed?` guard | serialize guard + `tx/publish!` under the wrapper lock — review P1(4) |\n   315\t| `:926`, `:969` `index/snapshot-datoms local-stream` | code unchanged; the handle is now a `memory-log` |\n   316\t| `schema/transactor`'s `local-stream` argument | callers pass a `memory-log` handle |\n   317\t\n   318\tThree of these are the review's, and each deserves its reasoning:\n   319\t\n   320\t**Schema state must not advance on a failed append.** D3 makes `full`,\n   321\t`closed`, `invalid-value` and `transport-error` returned data. `schema/\n   322\ttransact!` currently does `result (tx/transact! …)` then `(reset! lock\n   323\tnext-state)` unconditionally, so under D3 alone schema would install a state\n   324\tdescribing a transaction that was never persisted — its uniqueness,\n   325\tlookup-ref, current-value and schema-epoch state diverging from the log. The\n   326\trule: install `next-state` **only** when the outcome is `:dao.stream/ok`;\n   327\treturn every conforming non-ok outcome unchanged with state untouched; leave\n   328\tstate unchanged when the inner call throws, as today. This is forced by D3, so\n   329\tit is inside the constraint, not schema's own migration.\n   330\t\n   331\t**`SchemaWrapper.close!` keeps returning `{:woke []}`.** It remains a v1\n   332\tprotocol method until schema's own plan; returning `tx/close!`'s\n   333\t`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}` would silently change a v1 public result\n   334\tin a namespace this plan is not migrating.\n   335\t\n   336\t**`schema/publish!`'s guard moves under the lock.** Today it checks\n   337\t`ds/closed?` outside the wrapper lock and then calls `tx/publish!`: a\n   338\tconcurrent close can land between the two, which is exactly the stale-predicate\n   339\tproblem cited to justify removing `closed?`. Of the review's two remedies —\n   340\tserialize, or redefine publication-after-close as permitted — take\n   341\t**serialize**. Redefining it is a semantic change to schema's documented\n   342\townership model, and that belongs to schema's own plan; serializing preserves\n   343\ttoday's behaviour exactly using a lock that already exists. The cost is real\n   344\tand worth stating: the wrapper lock is now held across an index build. On a\n   345\tsingle-writer wrapper a concurrent `transact!` would serialize against the\n   346\tpublish's snapshot regardless, so the throughput change is smaller than it\n   347\tlooks.\n   348\t\n   349\t### D9 — the local-log wiring requirement\n   350\t\n   351\tThe handoff, now true rather than aspirational:\n   352\t\n   353\t> The host composition supplies `dao.space` a local handle created by\n   354\t> `dao.stream.memory-log/create!`. Its declared complete retention makes\n   355\t> fresh `:oldest` cursors true origin cursors for `derive-next-t` and\n   356\t> `publish-index!`; supplying an evicting transport is a host-assembly defect.\n   357\t\n   358\tThis is a **declaration, not a check** (D2, D6). It goes in\n   359\t`dao.space.index.md`, `dao.space.md`'s *The Write Path*, and the transactor's\n   360\town design section, and it is the reason S5 and T4's gap half retire.\n   361\t\n   362\t---\n   363\t\n   364\t## 2. The invariants this plan is accountable to\n   365\t\n   366\t### Transactor\n   367\t\n   368\t| # | Invariant | |\n   369\t|---|---|---|\n   370\t| T1 | Every `append!`/`transact!` writes exactly ONE atomic transaction record `{:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms […]}}` through exactly one local append, so no reader observes a torn transaction | `[D]` |\n   371\t| T2 | `t` comes from a per-wrapper watermark, derived on open as 0 for empty history else 1 + max datom `t` | `[D]` |\n   372\t| T3 | A caller-supplied `:next-t` is rejected | `[D]` |\n   373\t| T4a | A malformed retained history fails the open | `[D]` |\n   374\t| T4b | A retention gap fails the open | **`[D✗]`** — the local stream's transport excludes `gap`. Replaced by T18. Its fixture goes with it (§7). |\n   375\t| T5 | The watermark advances **iff** the local append succeeded; a failed or thrown append leaves the same `t` retryable | `[D]` + `[T]` :499, :515 — strengthened by D3 from prose to a returned outcome |\n   376\t| T6 | Single-writer: two wrappers over one local stream derive the same `t` and write colliding records; a documented hazard, not silently coordinated | `[D]` + `[T]` :655 |\n   377\t| T7 | Close is per handle: it rejects further writes and neither closes nor erases the local stream or the intake pool | `[D]` + `[T]` :584 |\n   378\t| T8 | Close linearizes after an in-flight append — no write crosses the point at which close returns | `[T]` :616 → `[T→D]` |\n   379\t| T9 | Opening creates, registers, or closes nothing | `[T]` :273 → `[T→D]` |\n   380\t| T10 | Entity maps require `:db/id`; an explicit datom `t` is rejected; `[e a v]`/`[e a v nil m]` pad to canonical d5 and are validated | `[D]` |\n   381\t| T11 | `publish!` passes the local stream, pool and opts to `index/publish-index!` and returns its result; publication acknowledges enqueuing only | `[D]` |\n   382\t| T12 | The wrapper delegates reads | **`[T✗]`** — pure delegation to a handle the caller holds; only tests call it |\n   383\t| T13 | `close!` returns `{:woke []}` | **`[T✗]`** for the transactor — v2 has no waiter registration. `SchemaWrapper.close!` keeps it (D8). |\n   384\t| T14 | `closed?` is a public predicate | **`[T✗]`** — v2 *Explicitly Absent*; replaced by the `:dao.stream/closed` outcome on the next write |\n   385\t| T15 | An append receipt carries ok, the allocated `t`, and the datoms | `[T]` → preserved in v2 vocabulary |\n   386\t| T16 | Intake pool members are validated with v2 `stream/writer?`; a non-empty collection is required | `[D]` |\n   387\t| T17 | A non-ok local append throws | **`[T✗]`** — returned as data (D3) |\n   388\t| T18 | The local stream is on a transport declaring complete retention; supplying an evicting one is a host assembly defect | **`[D]`, new** — declared, never checked (D9) |\n   389\t| T19 | Schema installs its next state only when the transactor answered ok | **`[D]`, new** — review P1(2) |\n   390\t\n   391\t### Index\n   392\t\n   393\t**Snapshot** — S1 the read begins at the logical sequence's origin, because\n   394\tthe transport declares complete retention `[D]`, restated from r2's \"reads\n   395\tfrom cursor zero\"; S2 an element is a canonical d5 vector or a transaction\n   396\trecord `[D]`; S3 the record's exact shape `[T→D]`; S4 `blocked`/`end` finish\n   397\tat the tail `[D]`; **S5 a gap throws before any emission `[D✗]`** — the\n   398\ttransport excludes `gap`, and per `conformance.cljc` a fixture inducing it\n   399\twould contradict the exclusion it is meant to support; S6 malformed anything\n   400\tthrows before any emission `[D]`, now also covering the totality branch; S7\n   401\tthe two distinct diagnostics `[T]`; S8 incremental flattening `[T✗]`, dropped\n   402\tin Phase 1; **S9 the local stream's transport declares complete retention\n   403\t`[D]`, new** — the same declaration as T18, stated on index because\n   404\t`publish-index!` depends on it independently of the transactor.\n   405\t\n   406\t**Published** — P1 the coordinate's exact shape `[D]`; P2 plain EDN, survives\n   407\ta stream round-trip `[T→D]`; P3 opening fetches exactly one blob, zero nodes\n   408\tfaulted `[D]`; P4 rows deferred, EAVT order, equal to `read-datoms` `[D]`; P5\n   409\ta store opened during a failed open is closed before the error propagates\n   410\t`[D]` — **and has no covering test today**, review P1(5), fixed in Phase 3;\n   411\tP6 the v1 adapter's reader/bound/not-writer, `closed?`-from-construction and\n   412\t`{:woke []}` close `[T]` — v1 protocol vocabulary that dies with the adapter;\n   413\tP7 two independent cursors over one realization `[T✗]`; P8 `covered-indexes`\n   414\tis a structural check, never an instance check `[D]`.\n   415\t\n   416\t---\n   417\t\n   418\t## 3. Phase 1 — split index's payload vocabulary from its reading\n   419\t\n   420\t`src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc` and its test only; no behaviour change. It\n   421\tlands first because Phase 2 needs the vocabulary separable from the reader.\n   422\t\n   423\t1. Rename private `stream-payload-datoms` → private `element-datoms`. Body\n   424\t   unchanged; S3, S6, S7 preserved verbatim including both error messages.\n   425\t2. Add public `datoms-from-elements`: `(into [] (mapcat element-datoms)\n   426\t   elements)`. `into` is eager, so S6's throw-before-emission ordering is\n   427\t   unchanged.\n   428\t3. `snapshot-datoms` becomes drain-then-flatten over the *existing v1 loop*:\n   429\t   accumulate `(:ok result)` payloads, then `datoms-from-elements` on the\n   430\t   `:blocked`/`:end` exit. Gap and malformed-signal branches unchanged in this\n   431\t   phase — they die in Phase 2. S8 is dropped here, with its reason.\n   432\t\n   433\t**Proof.** Add `datoms-from-elements-is-the-local-stream-vocabulary` to\n   434\t`index_test`, pinning S2, S3, S6, S7 directly rather than only through\n   435\t`publish-index!`: a bare datom passes through; a transaction record flattens;\n   436\textra keys / non-integer `:t` / empty `:datoms` / a datom whose `t` disagrees\n   437\twith the record all throw; a 5-vector with a non-namespaced `a` gets\n   438\t\"malformed local datom\"; anything else gets \"must be a datom or dao.space\n   439\ttransaction record\". Every existing snapshot test stays as written and now\n   440\tpins the drain and the composition rather than the vocabulary.\n   441\t\n   442\t**Docs.** None — nothing public changes except one added function, documented\n   443\twith Phase 2's surface edit.\n   444\t\n   445\t## 4. Phase 2 — the swap\n   446\t\n   447\tOne phase: the `defopen` and `DaoStreamLog` cannot be half-deleted, and every\n   448\tconsumer breaks at the same instant.\n   449\t\n   450\t### 4.1 `src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc`\n   451\t\n   452\tDelete `DaoStreamLog`, `ds/defopen :transactor`, the `[dao.stream :as ds]`\n   453\trequire and the `#?(:cljs (:require-macros [dao.stream]))`.\n   454\t\n   455\t- `create!` per D2: validate the spec (reject `:next-t`; require a\n   456\t  `stream/reader?`-and-`stream/writer?` local stream; require a non-empty pool\n   457\t  of `stream/writer?` members — the check at :199-204 is already v2 and moves\n   458\t  verbatim), then `(derive-next-t (index/snapshot-datoms local-stream))` and\n   459\t  return `{:dao.space/transactor true :local-stream … :intake-pool … :name …\n   460\t  :next-t (atom t) :state (atom {:closed false})}`. A tag key, following\n   461\t  `:dao.space.query/published`. **No retention check** (D2).\n   462\t- `append-packet!` per D3: `stream/append!`, `stream/valid-outcome?`, advance\n   463\t  on `:dao.stream/ok` only, return the local outcome as data otherwise.\n   464\t- `append!` / `transact!` become plain functions over the value, keeping\n   465\t  `with-write-lock`, `val->datoms`, `entity->datoms`, `pad-datom` and\n   466\t  `derive-next-t` exactly as they are. The closed check returns\n   467\t  `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}` instead of throwing.\n   468\t- `close!` sets `:closed` inside the write lock and returns\n   469\t  `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}`; idempotent.\n   470\t- `publish!` reads `(:local-stream log)` / `(:intake-pool log)` instead of\n   471\t  deftype fields.\n   472\t- No `next`, no `closed?`.\n   473\t\n   474\t### 4.2 `src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc`\n   475\t\n   476\t`snapshot-datoms` becomes the v2 read of D6 — three branches, no gap branch,\n   477\tone totality throw. It uses `stream/cursor` and `stream/next` directly; **no\n   478\t`observe` require** (D5, D7). `publish-index!`'s signature is unchanged; its\n   479\t`local-stream` is now a `memory-log` handle. The `ds` require survives this\n   480\tphase for the published adapter only, and dies in Phase 3.\n   481\t\n   482\t### 4.3 `src/cljc/dao/space/schema.cljc`\n   483\t\n   484\tThe seven forced edits in D8's table, nothing else. Explicitly untouched:\n   485\t`ds/defopen :dao.space.schema/current` and its opener, `ds/strict-vec` at\n   486\t:260, `ds/realization?` at :259/:318, and `schema_test:304`'s v1 realization\n   487\tfixture.\n   488\t\n   489\t### 4.4 Tests — the closed inventory (review P1(3))\n   490\t\n   491\tr2 named three doubles. The complete set in `transactor_test`, all of which\n   492\trepresent local handles and all of which fail the new v2 surface validation if\n   493\tleft on v1:\n   494\t\n   495\t| construct | line | becomes |\n   496\t|---|---|---|\n   497\t| `ReaderOnlyStream` | :25 | v2 reader only — still a negative fixture for `create!`'s surface check |\n   498\t| `WriterOnlyStream` | :33 | v2 writer only — same |\n   499\t| `RecordingAppendStream` | :41 | v2 reader+writer wrapping a `memory-log`, recording appends |\n   500\t| `FailingAppendStream` | :62 | v2 writer answering `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/full}` then delegating |\n   501\t| `ThrowingAppendStream` | :86 | v2 writer that throws once, then delegates |\n   502\t| concurrency `slow-local` reify | :401 | v2 reader+writer over a `memory-log`, `Thread/sleep` retained |\n   503\t| close-linearization reify | :617 | v2 reader+writer over a `memory-log`, promises retained |\n   504\t\n   505\tPlus, exhaustively: every `ds/append!`, `ds/close!`, `ds/closed?` and\n   506\t`ds/next` whose target is the transactor value becomes the corresponding\n   507\t`transactor/` function; `readers-observe-atomic-transaction-records` (:382)\n   508\tand the read assertion at :609 read `local` rather than `log` (T12); `tx-ts`\n   509\tand every `ds/->seq`/`ds/strict-vec` local-stream helper become one\n   510\t`stream-values` loop, generalizing the existing `intake-values` (:126); all 22\n   511\t`{:dao.stream/type :ringbuffer}` local opens become `memory-log/create!`;\n   512\t`descriptor-validation` becomes `spec-validation` with the constructor's\n   513\twording; and the namespace docstring's `ds/append!` prose is rewritten.\n   514\t\n   515\t**The residue check is the closure criterion**, not the list:\n   516\t`grep -c \"ds/\" test/dao/space/transactor_test.cljc` must return **0**\n   517\t(currently 135). The same check applies to `query_test` once its `open-local`\n   518\t(:567) moves — currently 2.\n   519\t\n   520\tElsewhere: `schema_test:658`'s local, `stigmergy_test:106`'s agent local,\n   521\t`index_test`'s `open-local` (:68) and `query_test`'s `open-local` (:567) all\n   522\tbecome `memory-log/create!`.\n   523\t\n   524\t### 4.5 What the deleted ring-buffer accounting becomes (review P1(6))\n   525\t\n   526\tr2's §6 is gone. The corrected accounting, with the review's correction taken:\n   527\t\n   528\t- **28** unbounded `ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :ringbuffer}` sites, not 29.\n   529\t  `query_test:97` is a literal map passed to `q` to prove a raw map is rejected\n   530\t  as a db input — not an open, and unchanged.\n   531\t- `schema_test:304` stays v1: it builds a closed v1 realization for\n   532\t  `schema/current`'s borrowed-input path, which is schema's own surface.\n   533\t- The other **27 migrate**, and r2 contradicted itself about one of them:\n   534\t  `index_test:777`'s descriptor carrier is **migrated to a v2 carrier**, not\n   535\t  deleted, because Phase 3 keeps that coverage. It is the one migrated site\n   536\t  that becomes a **ring buffer** rather than a memory-log — the point of the\n   537\t  test is that a coordinate is ordinary data on an ordinary stream, and\n   538\t  nothing about a carrier needs complete retention.\n   539\t- `index_test:379`'s capacity-bearing gap fixture is **deleted** with S5 (§7).\n   540\t- Every other migrated site is a `dao.space` local stream and becomes\n   541\t  `memory-log/create!`. There is no capacity to choose: the transport declares\n   542\t  no capacity key, and passing `:dao.stream.memory-log/capacity` is\n   543\t  `invalid-spec` by construction.\n   544\t\n   545\t### 4.6 Proof\n   546\t\n   547\tThe unchanged deftests are the proof: `reopen-derives-next-t-from-retained-\n   548\thistory` (:291) for T2, the three malformed sub-cases of\n   549\t`malformed-or-gapped-retained-history-throws` (:336, :344, :352) for T4a,\n   550\t`each-transaction-is-exactly-one-append` (:366) for T1,\n   551\t`append-failure-does-not-advance-t-and-can-retry` (:499) and\n   552\t`append-throw-does-not-advance-t-and-can-retry` (:515) for T5 — the first now\n   553\tobserving a returned outcome instead of a catch —\n   554\t`close-is-per-handle-and-does-not-touch-local-stream` (:584) for T7,\n   555\t`close-linearizes-after-an-in-flight-append` (:616) for T8,\n   556\t`single-writer-wrappers-are-not-coordinated` (:655) for T6,\n   557\t`publish-enqueues-indexes-into-the-pool` (:681) for T11.\n   558\t\n   559\tThree new tests:\n   560\t\n   561\t1. **T14's replacement** — `append!` and `transact!` after `close!` each answer\n   562\t   `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}` rather than throwing.\n   563\t2. **T19, in `schema_test`** (review P1(2)) — a `memory-log`-backed writer\n   564\t   double that answers `full` once then succeeds. Assert: after the failed\n   565\t   `schema/transact!`, the wrapper's schema/uniqueness state is byte-identical\n   566\t   to before; the returned value is the conforming non-ok outcome; and the\n   567\t   retry commits at the **same** `t`. This is the only way to exercise D3's\n   568\t   non-ok path, since the real transport excludes `full`.\n   569\t3. **T8's lock extension** (review P1(4)) — `schema/publish!` while a close is\n   570\t   in flight resolves one way or the other, never publishing after the close\n   571\t   returned. JVM-only, alongside the existing linearization test.\n   572\t\n   573\t### 4.7 Docs, in-phase\n   574\t\n   575\t- `docs/design/dao.space.index.md` — replace the `ds/open! {:dao.stream/type\n   576\t  :transactor …}` sample (:195-210) and the descriptor sentence (:191) with\n   577\t  `memory-log/create!` + `transactor/create!`; add `datoms-from-elements` to\n   578\t  the public surface; restate *The snapshot* on the transport's declared\n   579\t  retention (S1, S9) and delete the gap-aborts-publication sentence (S5);\n   580\t  quote D9's handoff paragraph.\n   581\t- `docs/design/dao.space.md` — *The Write Path* sample at :509, the descriptor\n   582\t  mention at :476, and D9's handoff.\n   583\t- `docs/design/dao.space.schema.md` — :383's \"opens the inner `:transactor`\"\n   584\t  becomes a transactor *value*, and the wrapper owns its own closedness.\n   585\t- `docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md` (review P2) — step 1's \"each agent owns a local\n   586\t  `dao.stream`\" becomes a `memory-log`, and step 2's `ds/append!`/`:transactor`\n   587\t  becomes `transactor/create!` and `transactor/append!`/`transact!`.\n   588\t- `docs/design/adr/0003-dao-space-is-the-event-medium.md` (review P2) — an\n   589\t  **amendment note**, not a supersession. The decision (dao.space is the event\n   590\t  medium; boundaries deposit into it) is untouched; two *Consequences* bullets\n   591\t  go stale: `DaoStreamLog` no longer exists and the deposit target is\n   592\t  `transactor/append!`, a plain function on a value, and the retention bullet's\n   593\t  \"`:dao.stream/full`, `:dao.stream/gap` so loss is never silent\" now needs the\n   594\t  pointer to *Complete history* — the local log excludes both, and its\n   595\t  completeness comes from the declaration rather than from those outcomes.\n   596\t- `docs/design/dao.stream.md` — drop `transactor` from the remaining-consumer\n   597\t  list; `index` goes in Phase 3.\n   598\t- A new `dao.space.transactor` section (or its own doc, owner's call) carrying\n   599\t  T1–T11, T15, T16, T18, T19, D3's outcome table, D9's handoff, and D4's\n   600\t  O(history) note as an Open item.\n   601\t\n   602\t## 5. Phase 3 — index closes\n   603\t\n   604\t*(r2's Phase 5, kept in full.)*\n   605\t\n   606\t### 5.1 What is built\n   607\t\n   608\tOne rewritten `defopen` body in `dao.space.schema`:\n   609\t\n   610\t```clojure\n   611\t(ds/defopen :dao.space.schema/published\n   612\t  [descriptor]\n   613\t  ;; validate exactly as today, then:\n   614\t  (let [store (jing-coordinate/open! content-store)\n   615\t        rows  (try (index/read-datoms store manifest-address)\n   616\t                   (finally (jing/close! store)))]\n   617\t    (->PublishedSchemaRows rows)))\n   618\t```\n   619\t\n   620\t`PublishedSchemaRows` is a small private record in `schema.cljc` implementing\n   621\t`ds/IDaoStreamReader` (`next` by position over the vector) and\n   622\t`ds/IDaoStreamBound` (`close!` → `{:woke []}`, `closed?` → true). This is\n   623\t*simpler* than what it replaces: schema forces the whole row vector at open\n   624\tanyway (`ds/strict-vec` at :1167), so nothing on this path ever needed the\n   625\tlazy restored trees or a retained store handle. The store closes inside the\n   626\topener, which also retires the comment at :1163-1166.\n   627\t\n   628\t### 5.2 What is deleted\n   629\t\n   630\t- `schema.cljc:1161` `ds/open! (index/published-index …)` and `:1167`\n   631\t  `ds/strict-vec`.\n   632\t- `index.cljc:344-363` `PublishedIndexStream`.\n   633\t- `index.cljc:383-404` `ds/defopen :dao.space.index/published`.\n   634\t- `index.cljc`'s `[dao.stream :as ds]` and `[dao.jing.coordinate]` requires.\n   635\t- `index_test`'s `[dao.stream :as ds]` require, after the accounting below.\n   636\t\n   637\t### 5.3 What Phase 3 must not touch\n   638\t\n   639\tThe edit is one `defopen` body. Out of bounds: `ds/defopen\n   640\t:dao.space.schema/current` (:357) and its whole opener; `SchemaWrapper`\n   641\t(:943-955) beyond D8's edits; `ds/strict-vec` at :260 and `ds/realization?` at\n   642\t:259/:318; `schema_test:304`'s v1 fixture; schema's closedness model beyond\n   643\tD8's table.\n   644\t\n   645\t### 5.4 The ten adapter call sites, per test\n   646\t\n   647\tTen call sites across eight deftests. A deleted test whose property is not\n   648\tcovered elsewhere is a defect, so each is accounted for:\n   649\t\n   650\t| # | `index_test` deftest (line, sites) | property | disposition |\n   651\t|---|---|---|---|\n   652\t| 1 | `open-published-rejects-unresolvable-and-malformed-descriptors` (:586; 600, 611) | an unsupported coordinate type fails closed at open; an extra key is rejected — P1 | **Moved.** Rewrite onto `query/open-published!` in `query_test`; nothing there covers coordinate rejection today. |\n   653\t| 2 | `open-published-rejects-missing-and-invalid-manifests` (:624; 637, 647) | a missing manifest address throws; a stored non-manifest throws | **Deleted.** Covered by `read-manifest-guards-missing-and-invalid` (:823), which pins both throws on the functions both openers call. |\n   654\t| 3 | `published-realization-is-read-only-with-a-stable-lifecycle` (:651; 656) | P6 and P7; EAVT order | **Deleted.** P6 is v1 protocol vocabulary that dies with the adapter; P7 is dropped. EAVT order survives in #7; idempotent close survives as `close-published-closes-once-and-is-idempotent` (`query_test`:715). |\n   655\t| 4 | `published-empty-index-drains-to-end` (:681; 683) | an empty manifest reads as no datoms, not an error | **Deleted, with one addition.** `publish-index-of-empty-input-is-readable` (:351) covers it via `read-datoms`; add one `query_test` case that `open-published!` on an empty manifest yields an empty relation. |\n   656\t| 5 | `published-open-fetches-only-the-manifest` (:690; 700) | exactly one content fetch at open, zero nodes faulted — P3 | **Moved.** `lazy-published-node-budget-is-strictly-bounded` (`query_test`:737) bounds *total* gets after a query at ≤ 4; it does not pin the open-time count. Add `open-published-fetches-only-the-manifest` asserting exactly 1 get after `open-published!` and before any read, reusing `counting-content-store`. |\n   657\t| 6 | `covered-indexes-returns-the-four-covered-sets` (:708; 710) | P8 | **Kept, minus the open.** The structural cases need no opened value and stay verbatim; the \"an opened realization carries the four sets\" case moves onto `query/open-published!`'s value, and that move is itself P8's proof. |\n   658\t| 7 | `published-next-yields-the-same-eavt-rows-as-the-eager-walk` (:736; 741) | P4 | **Moved.** `open-published!`'s forced `:rows` delay versus `index/read-datoms`. |\n   659\t| 8 | `published-index-is-a-transportable-bounded-stream` (:759; 783, carrier :777) | P2 | **Moved and modernized.** Keep the `pr-str`/`read-string` round-trip and the carrier, now a **v2 ring buffer** (§4.5), and open the transported coordinate with `query/open-published!`. Per the review, assert the **complete exact coordinate map**, not only `:manifest-address`. `ds/exact-bound?` (:779) is v1 and is replaced by that equality. |\n   660\t\n   661\t**Plus one test the adapter never had (review P1(5)):** P5 — \"a store opened\n   662\tduring a failed open is closed before the error propagates\" — has no covering\n   663\ttest anywhere, and the survivors above prove validation errors, not ownership\n   664\tcleanup. Add a `query/open-published!` test with a close-counting store whose\n   665\tmanifest read fails after `jing-coordinate/open!` succeeds; assert the original\n   666\terror propagates and the store is closed **exactly once**.\n   667\t\n   668\t### 5.5 `index_test`'s residue\n   669\t\n   670\tAfter Phase 2 migrates `open-local` (:68), `stream-values` (:122), the\n   671\tmalformed-signal reader double (:27-30) and the carrier (:777), and Phase 3\n   672\tremoves the eight deftests above and the deleted gap fixture (:375-392), the\n   673\tresidue is zero. `grep -c \"ds/\" test/dao/space/index_test.cljc` must return\n   674\t**0** (currently 41); a non-zero count means a property was migrated by hand\n   675\tinstead of by rule.\n   676\t\n   677\t### 5.6 Proof\n   678\t\n   679\t`schema_test` W38–W41 (:1449, :1481, :1522) must pass **unmodified** — they\n   680\texercise `schema/published` through `ds/open!`/`schema/current`, exactly the\n   681\tsurface Phase 3 preserves while replacing its implementation. If any needs\n   682\tediting, the opener's behaviour changed and the phase is wrong. `stigmergy_test`\n   683\tis the end-to-end check across both `:dao.jing/file` and `:dao.jing/remote`\n   684\tcoordinates.\n   685\t\n   686\t### 5.7 Docs, in-phase (review P2)\n   687\t\n   688\t- `docs/design/dao.space.index.md` :230-235 — the \"published-index DaoStream\n   689\t  adapter opens lazily\" bullet describes a deleted adapter. Rewrite on\n   690\t  `query/open-published!` and `read-datoms`; remove the adapter from *What the\n   691\t  library owns* and from the public surface list.\n   692\t- `docs/design/dao.space.schema.md` :549-554 — the paragraph explaining that\n   693\t  `:dao.space.schema/published` \"delegates to the `published-index`\n   694\t  realization … since `open!` dispatches on `:dao.stream/type` and the index\n   695\t  opener demands exact descriptor equality\" describes a mechanism that no\n   696\t  longer exists. Rewrite on `index/read-datoms`.\n   697\t- `docs/design/dao.stream.md` — drop `index` from the remaining-consumer list.\n   698\t\n   699\t## 6. End condition\n   700\t\n   701\t- **`dao.space.transactor` requires no `dao.stream`**: `dao.datom`,\n   702\t  `dao.space.index`, `dao.stream`.\n   703\t- **`dao.space.index` requires no `dao.stream`**: `dao.data.btree`,\n   704\t  `dao.data.btree.storage`, `dao.datom`, `dao.jing`, `dao.stream`.\n   705\t  Confirmed by grep (D7).\n   706\t- **`docs/design/dao.stream.md`'s consumer list drops both**, from\n   707\t  \"`dao.space` (index, schema, transactor — `query` migrated …)\" to\n   708\t  \"`dao.space` (`schema` only — `query`, `index` and `transactor` migrated,\n   709\t  and the `dao.stream.relation` transport was eliminated with `query`)\".\n   710\t- **`dao.stream.memory-log` gains its first production consumer.** It has\n   711\t  none today.\n   712\t- **`dao.space.schema` is the only `dao.space` namespace left on v1.** Its v1\n   713\t  surface, measured — the owner's earlier \"`ds/close!` ×4\" included the prose\n   714\t  mention at :1164, so it is 3 call sites:\n   715\t\n   716\t| API | today | after Phases 2+3 | remaining |\n   717\t|---|---|---|---|\n   718\t| `ds/open!` | 4 | 2 | :351, :363 |\n   719\t| `ds/closed?` | 4 | 3 | :323, :1069, :1127 |\n   720\t| `ds/close!` | 3 (+1 prose) | 2 | :367, :373 |\n   721\t| `ds/strict-vec` | 2 | 1 | :260 |\n   722\t| `ds/realization?` | 2 | 2 | :259, :318 |\n   723\t| `ds/defopen` | 2 | 2 | :357, :1154 (body replaced, form kept) |\n   724\t| `satisfies? ds/IDaoStream*` | 2 | 2 | :319, :364 |\n   725\t| `SchemaWrapper` protocol impl | 1 | 1 | :945 |\n   726\t| **total** | **20** | **15** | |\n   727\t\n   728\t  Plus one addition: Phase 3's `PublishedSchemaRows`. Worth naming rather than\n   729\t  hiding — Phase 3 **relocates** index's share of v1 into the one namespace\n   730\t  that still owes a plan, where schema's migration deletes the whole opener and\n   731\t  the record with it. A remainder belongs with its owner.\n   732\t- Nothing is owed to a later plan except schema's own migration, which was\n   733\t  always its own.\n   734\t\n   735\t## 7. What is deleted with S5 and T4b\n   736\t\n   737\tStated separately because deleting a test needs the same standard as keeping\n   738\tone, and here the standard is a contract commit rather than a survivor.\n   739\t\n   740\t`publish-index-gap-local-stream-throws-before-emission` (`index_test`:375-392)\n   741\tand the gap sub-case of `malformed-or-gapped-retained-history-throws`\n   742\t(`transactor_test`:323-335) both build a capacity-bearing ring buffer, overflow\n   743\tit, and assert a throw. Under D9 that fixture *is* the host-assembly defect the\n   744\tcontract now names, and `conformance.cljc`'s exclusion principle is explicit:\n   745\t\"Fixtures must never be required for excluded outcomes: a fixture inducing one\n   746\twould contradict the exclusion it is meant to support.\" The exclusion is\n   747\tdischarged by the structural argument in `memory-log`'s docstring — one dense\n   748\tvector from position 0, no expression that removes an element — not by a test\n   749\tin `dao.space`.\n   750\t\n   751\tThe malformed sub-cases of the same `transactor_test` deftest **stay**: any\n   752\tvalue can be appended to a memory-log, so a malformed payload or transaction\n   753\trecord in the retained history remains reachable, and T4a and S6 stand.\n   754\t\n   755\t## 8. Constraints\n   756\t\n   757\t- Three lanes green at the end of every phase.\n   758\t- `public/demo.html` verified by compiling `:demo`.\n   759\t- `dao.space.schema`'s own migration stays its own plan; this plan touches\n   760\t  schema only where D8's table and §5.1 say.\n   761\t\n   762\t## 9. Verification\n   763\t\n   764\t```\n   765\tbb test:clj\n   766\tbb test:cljs      # confirm \"Testing dao.space.transactor-test\", \"…index-test\",\n   767\t                  # \"…schema-test\" appear in the node output\n   768\tbb test:cljd\n   769\tclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile demo\n   770\t```\n   771\t\n   772\t- **Demo.** `datomworld.demo` requires only `datomworld.demo.*`, `reagent` and\n   773\t  `yin.vm.telemetry-viewer` — it reaches no `dao.space` namespace — so a break\n   774\t  would be a compile regression, not a behavioural one. Compile it each phase.\n   775\t- **Phase 2 is the likeliest red**, and the failure shape is now a receipt- or\n   776\t  surface-shape mismatch in the migrated sites rather than anything about\n   777\t  retention — the transport removed that class of failure instead of managing\n   778\t  it.\n   779\t- **`close-linearizes-after-an-in-flight-append` (:616) is the T8 canary**,\n   780\t  JVM-only. If it goes red against the v2 reify, the write lock stopped\n   781\t  covering the state transition.\n   782\t- **`schema_test` W38–W41 must pass unmodified through Phase 3** — §5.6.\n   783\t- **Four zero-residue greps**, which are the phases' closure criteria rather\n   784\t  than commentary: `ds/` in `transactor_test` (135 → 0), in `query_test`\n   785\t  (2 → 0), in `index_test` (41 → 0), and\n   786\t  `jing-coordinate/\\|ds/\\|observe/` in `index.cljc` (5 → 0).\n   787\t- **End-to-end**, `stigmergy_test.clj` covers the sweep: agents with\n   788\t  `memory-log` local streams, transaction records, publish, observer\n   789\t  materialization, and query over published manifests through file and remote\n   790\t  coordinates. It proves T1, T2, T11 and S1–S4 held; it does **not** prove P5,\n   791\t  which is why §5.4 adds that test explicitly — r2's claim that stigmergy\n   792\t  covered P1–P5 was too strong (review P1(5)).\n   793\t\n   794\t## 10. Review findings index\n   795\t\n   796\t| Finding | Where addressed |\n   797\t|---|---|\n   798\t| P0 — minting `:oldest` cannot detect evicted history | Accepted in full. Context; D4, D5, D6, D9; S1, S5, S9, T4b, T18; §4.5, §7 |\n   799\t| P1 — schema state advances on failed append | D8, T19, §4.3, §4.6 test 2 |\n   800\t| P1 — Phase 3 test inventory not closed | §4.4, with the residue greps as the closure criterion |\n   801\t| P1 — stale closedness predicate and unspecified return | D8 (both halves: `{:woke []}` preserved; `publish!` serialized under the lock, with the reason for choosing that remedy) |\n   802\t| P1 — P5 has no covering failure-path test | §5.4 final paragraph; §9's softened stigmergy claim |\n   803\t| P1 — capacity-site accounting contradicts Phase 5 | §4.5: 28 not 29, `query_test:97` is a literal, `schema_test:304` stays v1, 27 migrate **including** the carrier |\n   804\t| P2 — documentation closure and false order-independence | §4.7 and §5.7: the standalone doc phase is deleted and each code phase carries its own edits, including `docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md` and ADR 0003's amendment |\n   805\t| Review's additional note — transport test should assert the exact coordinate map | §5.4 row 8 |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1788874517712-review-space-transactor-index-plan.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md | sed -n '1,340p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc | sed -n '1,280p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc | sed -n '320,620p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/schema.cljc | sed -n '900,1175p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1788874517712-review-space-transactor-index-plan.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md | sed -n '1,340p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc | sed -n '1,280p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc | sed -n '320,620p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/schema.cljc | sed -n '900,1175p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tI’m using the repository’s architecture-review checklist, treating the plan’s phase boundaries and test-survivor table as executable contracts. I’ll trace compilation closure, wrapper readership, closedness semantics, capacity determinism, and every Phase 5 test move without running suites or changing files.\n     2\t\n     3\tThe plan’s architecture is internally coherent so far, but two areas need exact source-level proof: whether Phase 3 really migrates every constructor/read call in one compilable set, and whether the capacity regression test can logically detect a lowered shared constant. I’m tracing those now, along with the ten adapter test properties.\n     4\t\n     5\tI’m continuing the static architect review with the repository’s code-review checklist. I’m focusing the remaining pass on the v2 cursor semantics, Phase 5’s named test survivors, and whether Phase 3’s inventory is genuinely compilation-closed.\n     6\t\n     7\tTwo substantive issues are now confirmed: the retention test cannot work with an `:oldest` cursor minted after eviction, and Phase 3 does not state how `schema/transact!` handles the new non-OK result data. I’m checking the plan’s inventories for any similarly concrete contradictions before I rank the report.\n     8\t\n     9\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-08 13:43:02 GMT  \n    10\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-08 20:43:02 +0700 (Asia/Bangkok)  \n    11\tCoding-Agent: codex  \n    12\tSession-ID: 01a080de-1a15-7d23-9a35-4106b127e4f0\n    13\t\n    14\t# Architect review: transactor + index v2 migration plan\n    15\t\n    16\t## Verdict\n    17\t\n    18\t**Not ready to implement.** The plan has six blocking defects, including one foundational mismatch between its full-history invariant and DaoStream v2 cursor semantics. D1 itself is sound, and most of Phase 5’s adapter-test disposition is correct, but the plan needs another revision before code begins.\n    19\t\n    20\t## Findings\n    21\t\n    22\t### P0 — Minting `:oldest` cannot detect already-evicted history\n    23\t\n    24\tFiles and lines:\n    25\t\n    26\t- [plan](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1788874487255-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:156), especially lines 160–186, 278–283, and 424–475\n    27\t- [ringbuffer.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:55)\n    28\t- [dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:471)\n    29\t- [query_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/space/query_test.cljc:359)\n    30\t\n    31\t**Blocks the plan: yes.**\n    32\t\n    33\tThe plan says `derive-next-t` and index publication read from “position zero,” but its proposed `observe/snapshot` mints a fresh `:dao.stream/oldest` cursor. In v2, `:oldest` means the earliest position currently retained, not the beginning of the logical stream. After eviction, the ring buffer returns a cursor at `:first`; reading from it succeeds over the retained suffix and reports no gap.\n    34\t\n    35\tThe existing query test explicitly records this fact: a fresh `:oldest` cursor cannot observe a gap on a quiescent ring buffer. Its gap test therefore uses a scripted reader.\n    36\t\n    37\tConsequences:\n    38\t\n    39\t- A reopened transactor can derive `next-t` from an incomplete suffix without noticing lost history.\n    40\t- `publish-index!` can silently publish an incomplete index.\n    41\t- `retained-history-survives-reopen-at-capacity` cannot produce the promised gap by creating a second transactor after eviction.\n    42\t- The assertions at plan lines 424–430 and 462–475 are false under the committed v2 contract.\n    43\t\n    44\t**Concrete change:** choose and document an actual full-history mechanism. The cleanest v2-compatible option is to retain an origin cursor minted before the first append and pass it explicitly to full-history consumers. That likely requires:\n    45\t\n    46\t- An `observe/snapshot` arity accepting a caller-owned cursor.\n    47\t- The transactor spec to receive and retain the local history’s origin cursor.\n    48\t- `index/snapshot-datoms`/`publish-index!` to receive that cursor rather than minting `:oldest`.\n    49\t- The eviction test to mint the origin cursor before overflowing the buffer and then prove that replay reports a gap.\n    50\t\n    51\tAlternatively, require a transport that provably never evicts. A large ring-buffer capacity alone is not proof of full retention.\n    52\t\n    53\tThe control leg also needs a fixed required-history count. “Repeat at `local-capacity`” does not ensure that lowering the constant below some other test’s history size will fail.\n    54\t\n    55\t### P1 — Schema state advances when the transactor append fails\n    56\t\n    57\tFiles and lines:\n    58\t\n    59\t- [plan](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1788874487255-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:194), especially D6 line 209 and Phase 3 lines 379–380\n    60\t- [schema.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/schema.cljc:1099), especially lines 1112–1114\n    61\t\n    62\t**Blocks the plan: yes.**\n    63\t\n    64\tD3 changes `tx/transact!` so `full`, `closed`, `invalid-value`, and `transport-error` return as data. But `schema/transact!` currently installs `next-state` unconditionally after calling the inner transactor:\n    65\t\n    66\t```clojure\n    67\tresult (tx/transact! ...)\n    68\t(reset! lock next-state)\n    69\tresult\n    70\t```\n    71\t\n    72\tChanging only the receipt shape, as D6 says, leaves schema believing an unpersisted transaction succeeded. Its uniqueness, lookup-ref, current-value, and schema-epoch state can then diverge from the local log.\n    73\t\n    74\t**Concrete change:** Phase 3 must state that `schema/transact!`:\n    75\t\n    76\t- Installs `next-state` only when the result outcome is `:dao.stream/ok`.\n    77\t- Returns every conforming non-OK outcome unchanged without mutating wrapper state.\n    78\t- Continues to leave state unchanged when the inner call throws.\n    79\t\n    80\tAdd a schema test using a local writer that returns `full` before succeeding; assert wrapper state is unchanged after failure and that retry commits at the same transaction time.\n    81\t\n    82\t### P1 — Phase 3’s test migration inventory is not closed\n    83\t\n    84\tFiles and lines:\n    85\t\n    86\t- [plan](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1788874487255-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:382)\n    87\t- [transactor_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/space/transactor_test.cljc:25)\n    88\t\n    89\t**Blocks the plan: yes.**\n    90\t\n    91\tThe plan names only `FailingAppendStream`, `ThrowingAppendStream`, and the close-linearization `reify`. The test file also has migration-relevant:\n    92\t\n    93\t- `ReaderOnlyStream`\n    94\t- `WriterOnlyStream`\n    95\t- `RecordingAppendStream`\n    96\t- The concurrency `slow-local` reify\n    97\t- The close-linearization reify\n    98\t- Every `ds/append!`, `ds/close!`, `ds/closed?`, and `ds/next` call whose target is the transactor value\n    99\t- V1 sequence helpers used to inspect local streams\n   100\t\n   101\tLeaving the first three records or the concurrency reify on v1 makes them fail the new v2 reader/writer validation. Leaving protocol calls on `log` fails because D1 removes the protocols from the transactor.\n   102\t\n   103\t**Concrete change:** make Phase 3’s inventory exhaustive: migrate all five records/reifies that represent local handles, both JVM reifies, all transactor protocol invocations, the read helpers, namespace prose, and finally require zero `ds/` references in `transactor_test`. Apply the same zero-residue check to `query_test` once its last v1 `open-local` helper moves.\n   104\t\n   105\t### P1 — The schema closedness bridge still has stale-predicate behavior and an unspecified return contract\n   106\t\n   107\tFiles and lines:\n   108\t\n   109\t- [plan](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1788874487255-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:194)\n   110\t- [schema.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/schema.cljc:942)\n   111\t- [schema.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/schema.cljc:1121)\n   112\t\n   113\t**Blocks the plan: yes.**\n   114\t\n   115\tHolding schema’s legacy closed flag locally is reasonable while `SchemaWrapper` still implements v1. The details are incomplete:\n   116\t\n   117\t1. `SchemaWrapper.close!` remains a v1 protocol method and must keep returning `{:woke []}`. If it simply returns `tx/close!`’s result, its public result silently changes to `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}` before schema’s own migration.\n   118\t\n   119\t2. `schema/publish!` checks `ds/closed?` outside the wrapper lock, then calls `tx/publish!`. A concurrent close can return between those operations, after which publication still proceeds. That is precisely the stale-predicate problem cited to justify removing `closed?`.\n   120\t\n   121\tThe `schema/transact!` check is sound because it occurs inside the same lock used by close.\n   122\t\n   123\t**Concrete change:** explicitly preserve `{:woke []}` from the v1 wrapper close method, and either serialize the `publish!` closed check plus publication under the wrapper lock or explicitly redefine publication-after-close as permitted and remove the guard. Do not retain an operational check that can become stale before its action.\n   124\t\n   125\t### P1 — P5 has no covering failure-path test after Phase 5\n   126\t\n   127\tFiles and lines:\n   128\t\n   129\t- [plan](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1788874487255-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:285)\n   130\t- Phase 5 accounting at [plan](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1788874487255-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:545)\n   131\t- [query.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:245)\n   132\t- [query_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/space/query_test.cljc:715)\n   133\t\n   134\t**Blocks the plan: yes.**\n   135\t\n   136\tP5 says a store opened during a failed open is closed before rethrow. The existing query tests cover successful explicit close and idempotence, but none makes `read-manifest` fail after `jing-coordinate/open!` succeeds and then observes the store being closed.\n   137\t\n   138\tThe direct missing/invalid-manifest tests proposed as the survivor for table entry #2 prove validation errors, not ownership cleanup. The successful stigmergy flow cannot prove this failure path, so the statement at plan line 649 that it covers P1–P5 is too strong.\n   139\t\n   140\t**Concrete change:** add a `query/open-published!` test with a close-counting store whose manifest read fails. Assert the original error propagates and the newly opened store is closed exactly once.\n   141\t\n   142\tThe other Phase 5 dispositions are sound: coordinate rejection moves correctly; empty input needs the stated opened-path test; open-time fetch count must move; P6/P7 may be dropped; P8 and eager/lazy parity have valid survivors; and coordinate transport belongs on a v2 carrier. The transport test should assert the complete exact coordinate map, not only `:manifest-address`.\n   143\t\n   144\t### P1 — Capacity-site accounting contradicts Phase 5\n   145\t\n   146\tFiles and lines:\n   147\t\n   148\t- [plan](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1788874487255-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:432)\n   149\t- Phase 5 table at [plan](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1788874487255-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:558)\n   150\t\n   151\t**Blocks the plan: yes.**\n   152\t\n   153\tThere are 28 actual unbounded `ds/open!` ring-buffer sites, plus the raw map in `query_test:97`, which is not an open. The plan calls all 29 “opens.”\n   154\t\n   155\tMore importantly, it says `index_test:777`’s carrier “must not be migrated” and is deleted outright, while Phase 5 correctly says to retain that coverage using a v2 carrier. The carrier therefore is migrated, not deleted.\n   156\t\n   157\tThe correct accounting is:\n   158\t\n   159\t- 28 unbounded v1 opens.\n   160\t- `schema_test:304` remains v1.\n   161\t- The other 27 migrate to v2, including the Phase 5 transport carrier.\n   162\t- `query_test:97` is merely an additional literal occurrence and remains unchanged.\n   163\t\n   164\tThis must be corrected so the capacity audit and zero-residue checks agree.\n   165\t\n   166\t### P2 — Documentation closure is incomplete and the claimed phase ordering is false\n   167\t\n   168\tFiles and lines:\n   169\t\n   170\t- [plan](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1788874487255-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:478)\n   171\t- [dao.space.stigmergy.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md:240)\n   172\t- [ADR 0003](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/adr/0003-dao-space-is-the-event-medium.md:39)\n   173\t- [dao.space.index.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.space.index.md:230)\n   174\t- [dao.space.schema.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.space.schema.md:550)\n   175\t\n   176\t**Blocks the plan: yes, because the plan says it leaves nothing owed.**\n   177\t\n   178\t`docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md` still instructs agents to open `:transactor` and call `ds/append!`; it is absent from Phase 4.\n   179\t\n   180\tADR 0003 names `DaoStreamLog`, says it implements `IDaoStreamWriter`, and calls repairing that writer face future work. As an accepted ADR it should at least receive an amendment or supersession note explaining D1.\n   181\t\n   182\tPhase 5 says it “carries its own doc edits,” but no such edits are enumerated. Several index/schema passages describe the adapter and schema’s delegation to it. Consequently Phases 4 and 5 are not actually order-independent: one order leaves documentation temporarily stale, and the other makes it prematurely describe code that has not landed.\n   183\t\n   184\t**Concrete change:** enumerate Phase 5’s precise documentation edits, include the stigmergy document, state the ADR disposition, and either order documentation after Phase 5 or split the edits so each code phase ends with truthful documentation.\n   185\t\n   186\t## Verified and cleared\n   187\t\n   188\t- **D1 is valid:** no production consumer reads through the transactor wrapper. Its only `next` implementation is delegation, and its callers are tests. `SchemaWrapper` implements only `IDaoStreamBound`.\n   189\t- The plain-value constructor and removal of ambient v1 dispatch are architecturally consistent with DaoStream v2.\n   190\t- D3’s transactor-level rule—validate the local append result, advance the watermark only on `ok`, return operational failures as data—is sound.\n   191\t- The stated reasons for dropping T12, T13, T14, T17, S8, P6, and P7 are legitimate rather than accidental coverage loss.\n   192\t- Phase 5’s adapter consumer inventory is closed: outside the adapter itself, current production use is schema’s published opener; remaining direct opens are the enumerated index tests.\n   193\t- The proposed direct schema opener closes its store in `finally` and preserves eager schema realization.\n   194\t- No new reader-conditional portability trap is specified. The proposed production changes can be expressed across clj/cljs/cljd; JVM-only synchronization tests remain correctly isolated.\n   195\t- No tests were run, as requested.\n   196\t\n   197\t**Final judgment: revise before implementation.** The origin-cursor/full-retention design is the essential first correction; without it, T2, T4, S1, S5, and the capacity proof cannot all be true.\n     1\t(ns dao.space.transactor\n     2\t  \"The `:transactor` stream: a single-writer wrapper over an explicit local\n     3\t   `dao.stream` plus an explicit intake pool of `dao.stream` writers\n     4\t   (docs/design/dao.jing.md, Publication from an agent).\n     5\t\n     6\t   Descriptor:\n     7\t\n     8\t     {:dao.stream/type :transactor\n     9\t      :local-stream s   ; must satisfy IDaoStreamReader and IDaoStreamWriter;\n    10\t                        ; supplied, never created, registered, or closed\n    11\t      :intake-pool [p]  ; non-empty; every member satisfies the dao.stream\n    12\t                        ; writer surface (stream/writer?);\n    13\t                        ; supplied, never created, registered, or closed\n    14\t      :name n}          ; optional, diagnostic only\n    15\t\n    16\t   The transactor owns transaction time. Every append! or transact! writes\n    17\t   exactly ONE atomic transaction record to the local stream\n    18\t\n    19\t     {:dao.space/transaction {:t <nonnegative-integer> :datoms [...]}}\n    20\t\n    21\t   through exactly one ds/append!, so no reader can ever observe a torn\n    22\t   transaction. t is allocated from a per-wrapper watermark: on open the\n    23\t   retained history is scanned from cursor zero (index/snapshot-datoms) and\n    24\t   the next t is derived as 0 for an empty history or 1 + the maximum\n    25\t   integer datom t otherwise. A retention gap or malformed history throws.\n    26\t   The scan is the causality boundary; a caller-supplied next-t is never\n    27\t   accepted.\n    28\t\n    29\t   Single-writer: calls through one wrapper are serialized around timestamp\n    30\t   allocation and append. The watermark is per-wrapper state, so two wrappers over\n    31\t   the same local stream each derive the same next-t and silently write\n    32\t   colliding records. That is invalid and cannot be silently coordinated\n    33\t   without shared mutable state. Use one wrapper per local stream.\n    34\t\n    35\t   publish! builds the covered indexes over the local stream and enqueues\n    36\t   them into the intake pool (dao.space.index/publish-index!). Publication\n    37\t   is explicit and acknowledges enqueuing only, never observer\n    38\t   materialization.\n    39\t\n    40\t   Closing a wrapper is per handle: it rejects further appends/transacts but\n    41\t   neither closes nor erases the local stream.\"\n    42\t  (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\n    43\t            [dao.space.index :as index]\n    44\t            [dao.stream :as ds]\n    45\t            [dao.stream :as stream])\n    46\t  #?(:cljs (:require-macros [dao.stream])))\n    47\t\n    48\t\n    49\t(defn- entity->datoms\n    50\t  \"One datom per k/v pair, all sharing transaction time `t`. `:db/id` is\n    51\t   required: a durable log cannot mint per-batch tempids without colliding\n    52\t   across appends. Attribute order within one entity's own datoms is\n    53\t   whatever the host's map iteration yields — unspecified across platforms.\"\n    54\t  [m t]\n    55\t  (when-not (contains? m :db/id)\n    56\t    (throw (ex-info \"entity map requires :db/id\" {:entity m})))\n    57\t  (let [e (:db/id m)]\n    58\t    (mapv (fn [[a v]]\n    59\t            (let [d [e a v t datom/default-op]]\n    60\t              (when-not (datom/local-datom? d)\n    61\t                (throw (ex-info \"invalid persistent local datom\"\n    62\t                                {:datom d, :entity m})))\n    63\t              d))\n    64\t          (remove (fn [[a _v]] (= a :db/id)) m))))\n    65\t\n    66\t\n    67\t(defn- pad-datom\n    68\t  \"Pad [e a v] / [e a v t m] to the canonical 5-tuple. The transactor owns\n    69\t   transaction time: a non-nil explicit t is rejected, a nil t is stamped\n    70\t   with the allocated `t`. A nil m takes the default assertion op; an\n    71\t   explicit m (0, a retraction) is preserved.\"\n    72\t  [d t]\n    73\t  (let [[e a v dt dm] d]\n    74\t    (when-not (nil? dt)\n    75\t      (throw\n    76\t        (ex-info\n    77\t          \"the transactor owns transaction time: explicit datom t is rejected\"\n    78\t          {:datom d})))\n    79\t    (let [padded [e a v t (if (nil? dm) datom/default-op dm)]]\n    80\t      (when-not (datom/local-datom? padded)\n    81\t        (throw (ex-info \"invalid persistent local datom\"\n    82\t                        {:datom padded, :input d})))\n    83\t      padded)))\n    84\t\n    85\t\n    86\t(defn- val->datoms\n    87\t  [val t]\n    88\t  (cond\n    89\t    (map? val) (entity->datoms val t)\n    90\t    (and (vector? val) (<= 3 (count val) 5)) [(pad-datom val t)]\n    91\t    :else\n    92\t    (throw\n    93\t      (ex-info\n    94\t        \"a value must be an entity map or datom vector [e a v] / [e a v nil m]\"\n    95\t        {:val val}))))\n    96\t\n    97\t\n    98\t#_{:clj-kondo/ignore [:unused-binding]}\n    99\t\n   100\t\n   101\t(defn- with-write-lock\n   102\t  \"Serialize timestamp allocation and append on hosts with shared-memory\n   103\t   threads. ClojureScript and ClojureDart calls are synchronous within one\n   104\t   isolate, so the direct call has the same single-writer semantics.\"\n   105\t  [lock f]\n   106\t  #?(:clj (locking lock (f))\n   107\t     :default (f)))\n   108\t\n   109\t\n   110\t(defn- append-packet!\n   111\t  \"Append one atomic transaction record to the local stream. The watermark\n   112\t   advances only after the underlying ds/append! answers {:result :ok}; on\n   113\t   any other result or on a thrown error the wrapper keeps the same t and\n   114\t   the call can be retried. Returns {:result :ok, :t t, :datoms datoms}.\"\n   115\t  [local-stream next-t t datoms context]\n   116\t  (let [record {:dao.space/transaction {:t t, :datoms datoms}}\n   117\t        result (ds/append! local-stream record)]\n   118\t    (when-not (and (map? result) (= :ok (:result result)))\n   119\t      (throw (ex-info (str context \" stream append failed: \" (pr-str result))\n   120\t                      {:result result, :t t, :datoms datoms})))\n   121\t    (swap! next-t inc)\n   122\t    {:result :ok, :t t, :datoms datoms}))\n   123\t\n   124\t\n   125\t(defn- derive-next-t\n   126\t  \"Derive the next transaction time from a flattened retained history: 0 for\n   127\t   an empty history, else 1 + the maximum datom t. A datom t that is not a\n   128\t   non-negative integer is a malformed history and throws — this scan is the\n   129\t   causality boundary of the wrapper.\"\n   130\t  [datoms]\n   131\t  (if (empty? datoms)\n   132\t    0\n   133\t    (let [ts (mapv index/datom-t datoms)]\n   134\t      (when-not (every? #(and (integer? %) (<= 0 %)) ts)\n   135\t        (throw\n   136\t          (ex-info\n   137\t            \"malformed retained history: datom t must be a non-negative integer\"\n   138\t            {:history datoms})))\n   139\t      (inc (reduce max ts)))))\n   140\t\n   141\t\n   142\t(deftype DaoStreamLog\n   143\t  [local-stream intake-pool stream-name next-t state]\n   144\t\n   145\t  ds/IDaoStreamWriter\n   146\t\n   147\t  (append!\n   148\t    [_this val]\n   149\t    (with-write-lock\n   150\t      next-t\n   151\t      (fn []\n   152\t        (when (:closed @state)\n   153\t          (throw (ex-info \"Cannot append to closed stream\"\n   154\t                          {:name stream-name})))\n   155\t        (let [t @next-t\n   156\t              datoms (val->datoms val t)]\n   157\t          (when (empty? datoms)\n   158\t            (throw (ex-info \"append! produced no datoms\" {:val val})))\n   159\t          (append-packet! local-stream next-t t datoms \"append!\")))))\n   160\t\n   161\t\n   162\t  ds/IDaoStreamReader\n   163\t\n   164\t  (next [_this cursor] (ds/next local-stream cursor))\n   165\t\n   166\t\n   167\t  ds/IDaoStreamBound\n   168\t\n   169\t  (close!\n   170\t    [_this]\n   171\t    (with-write-lock next-t\n   172\t      (fn [] (swap! state assoc :closed true) {:woke []})))\n   173\t\n   174\t\n   175\t  (closed? [_this] (:closed @state)))\n   176\t\n   177\t\n   178\t(ds/defopen\n   179\t  :transactor\n   180\t  [descriptor]\n   181\t  (let [{:keys [local-stream intake-pool name]} descriptor\n   182\t        stream-name (or name \"transactor\")]\n   183\t    (when (contains? descriptor :next-t)\n   184\t      (throw\n   185\t        (ex-info\n   186\t          \":transactor derives transaction time from retained history; :next-t is not accepted\"\n   187\t          {:descriptor descriptor})))\n   188\t    (when-not (and (satisfies? ds/IDaoStreamReader local-stream)\n   189\t                   (satisfies? ds/IDaoStreamWriter local-stream))\n   190\t      (throw\n   191\t        (ex-info\n   192\t          \":transactor descriptor requires a :local-stream satisfying IDaoStreamReader and IDaoStreamWriter\"\n   193\t          {:descriptor descriptor})))\n   194\t    (when-not (and (coll? intake-pool) (seq intake-pool))\n   195\t      (throw\n   196\t        (ex-info\n   197\t          \":transactor descriptor requires a non-empty :intake-pool of streams satisfying the dao.stream writer surface\"\n   198\t          {:descriptor descriptor})))\n   199\t    (doseq [intake intake-pool]\n   200\t      (when-not (stream/writer? intake)\n   201\t        (throw\n   202\t          (ex-info\n   203\t            \":transactor :intake-pool members must satisfy IDaoStreamWriter\"\n   204\t            {:intake-pool intake-pool}))))\n   205\t    (let [next-t (derive-next-t (index/snapshot-datoms local-stream))]\n   206\t      (->DaoStreamLog local-stream\n   207\t                      intake-pool\n   208\t                      stream-name\n   209\t                      (atom next-t)\n   210\t                      (atom {:closed false})))))\n   211\t\n   212\t\n   213\t(defn publish!\n   214\t  \"Build and enqueue the covered indexes over the local stream into the\n   215\t   intake pool (dao.space.index/publish-index!), returning its\n   216\t   {:manifest-address ... :manifest ...}. opts are passed through to\n   217\t   publish-index! (e.g. :branching-factor, :select-stream). Publication is\n   218\t   explicit and acknowledges enqueuing only, never observer\n   219\t   materialization.\"\n   220\t  ([log] (publish! log nil))\n   221\t  ([^DaoStreamLog log opts]\n   222\t   (index/publish-index! (.-local-stream log) (.-intake-pool log) opts)))\n   223\t\n   224\t\n   225\t(defn transact!\n   226\t  \"Commits a non-empty collection of entity maps or datom vectors as a\n   227\t  single atomic transaction: every datom shares one allocated t and lands\n   228\t   in one transaction record through exactly one ds/append!, so no partial\n   229\t   prefix is possible. Throws when the stream is closed, the collection is\n   230\t   empty, any item is invalid, or the expansion yields no datoms.\"\n   231\t  [^DaoStreamLog log tx-data]\n   232\t  (when (empty? tx-data)\n   233\t    (throw (ex-info \"transact! requires at least one transaction item\"\n   234\t                    {:tx-data tx-data})))\n   235\t  (let [next-t (.-next-t log)]\n   236\t    (with-write-lock\n   237\t      next-t\n   238\t      (fn []\n   239\t        (when (ds/closed? log)\n   240\t          (throw (ex-info \"Cannot transact! to closed stream\"\n   241\t                          {:name (.-stream-name log)})))\n   242\t        (let [t @next-t\n   243\t              datoms (into [] (mapcat #(val->datoms % t)) tx-data)]\n   244\t          (when (empty? datoms)\n   245\t            (throw (ex-info \"transact! produced no datoms\" {:tx-data tx-data})))\n   246\t          (append-packet! (.-local-stream log) next-t t datoms \"transact!\"))))))\n   320\t\n   321\t\n   322\t;; =============================================================================\n   323\t;; Published covered index as a bounded logical d5 stream\n   324\t;; =============================================================================\n   325\t\n   326\t(defn published-index\n   327\t  \"Construct a transportable descriptor for one immutable covered-index\n   328\t   manifest. `content-store` is a serializable DaoJing coordinate, not a live\n   329\t   handle. The manifest address is the exact stream bound.\"\n   330\t  [content-store manifest-address]\n   331\t  (when-not (and (map? content-store) (keyword? (:dao.jing/type content-store)))\n   332\t    (throw (ex-info \"published index requires a DaoJing store coordinate\"\n   333\t                    {:content-store content-store})))\n   334\t  (when-not (jing/segment-address? manifest-address)\n   335\t    (throw (ex-info \"published index requires a manifest content address\"\n   336\t                    {:manifest-address manifest-address})))\n   337\t  {:dao.stream/type :dao.space.index/published,\n   338\t   :dao.stream/bound {:manifest-address manifest-address},\n   339\t   :dao.stream/comparator :dao.space.index/eavt,\n   340\t   :content-store content-store,\n   341\t   :manifest-address manifest-address})\n   342\t\n   343\t\n   344\t(defrecord PublishedIndexStream\n   345\t  [descriptor datoms indexes store]\n   346\t\n   347\t  ds/IDaoStreamReader\n   348\t\n   349\t  (next\n   350\t    [_ cursor]\n   351\t    (let [rows (force datoms)\n   352\t          position (or (:position cursor) 0)]\n   353\t      (if (< position (count rows))\n   354\t        {:ok (nth rows position), :cursor {:position (inc position)}}\n   355\t        :end)))\n   356\t\n   357\t\n   358\t  ds/IDaoStreamBound\n   359\t\n   360\t  (close! [_] (jing/close! store) {:woke []})\n   361\t\n   362\t\n   363\t  (closed? [_] true))\n   364\t\n   365\t\n   366\t(defn covered-indexes\n   367\t  \"Return the {:eavt :aevt :avet :vaet} covered d5 sets carried in a\n   368\t   realization's :indexes field, or nil when the realization carries none.\n   369\t   Structural check only: the realization must be a map whose :indexes field\n   370\t   is a map with exactly those four keys, so it is clj/cljs/cljd-portable and\n   371\t   never an instance/type check. It means the realization carries covered d5\n   372\t   sets, not that it is lazy: an in-memory index-datoms map is structurally\n   373\t   indistinguishable, and that is fine (resolution happens the same way\n   374\t   either way).\"\n   375\t  [realization]\n   376\t  (when (map? realization)\n   377\t    (let [indexes (:indexes realization)]\n   378\t      (when (and (map? indexes)\n   379\t                 (= #{:eavt :aevt :avet :vaet} (set (keys indexes))))\n   380\t        indexes))))\n   381\t\n   382\t\n   383\t(ds/defopen :dao.space.index/published\n   384\t            [descriptor]\n   385\t            (let [{:keys [content-store manifest-address]} descriptor\n   386\t                  expected (published-index content-store manifest-address)]\n   387\t              (when-not (= expected descriptor)\n   388\t                (throw (ex-info \"invalid published-index descriptor\"\n   389\t                                {:descriptor descriptor, :expected expected})))\n   390\t              (let [store (jing-coordinate/open! content-store)]\n   391\t                (try (let [manifest (read-manifest store manifest-address)\n   392\t                           indexes (restored-indexes store manifest)]\n   393\t                       (with-meta (->PublishedIndexStream\n   394\t                                    descriptor\n   395\t                                    (delay (vec (bt/seq (:eavt indexes))))\n   396\t                                    indexes\n   397\t                                    store)\n   398\t                         {:dao.stream/descriptor descriptor}))\n   399\t                     (catch #?(:clj Throwable\n   400\t                               :cljs :default\n   401\t                               :cljd Object)\n   402\t                            error\n   403\t                       (jing/close! store)\n   404\t                       (throw error))))))\n   405\t\n   406\t\n   407\t;; =============================================================================\n   408\t;; publish-index! — build, record, append\n   409\t;; =============================================================================\n   410\t\n   411\t(defn- stream-payload-datoms\n   412\t  [payload]\n   413\t  (if (and (map? payload) (contains? payload :dao.space/transaction))\n   414\t    (let [tx (:dao.space/transaction payload)\n   415\t          t (:t tx)\n   416\t          datoms (:datoms tx)]\n   417\t      (when-not (and (map? tx)\n   418\t                     (= #{:t :datoms} (set (keys tx)))\n   419\t                     (integer? t)\n   420\t                     (not (neg? t))\n   421\t                     (vector? datoms)\n   422\t                     (seq datoms)\n   423\t                     (every? datom/local-datom? datoms)\n   424\t                     (every? #(= t (datom-t %)) datoms))\n   425\t        (throw (ex-info \"malformed dao.space transaction record\"\n   426\t                        {:payload payload})))\n   427\t      datoms)\n   428\t    (cond\n   429\t      (datom/local-datom? payload) [payload]\n   430\t      (and (vector? payload) (= 5 (count payload)))\n   431\t      (throw\n   432\t        (ex-info\n   433\t          \"malformed local datom: e and t must be non-negative integers, m an integer, and a a namespaced keyword\"\n   434\t          {:datom payload}))\n   435\t      :else\n   436\t      (throw\n   437\t        (ex-info\n   438\t          \"local stream payload must be a datom or dao.space transaction record\"\n   439\t          {:payload payload})))))\n   440\t\n   441\t\n   442\t(defn snapshot-datoms\n   443\t  \"Eagerly snapshot an agent-local stream by walking `{:position 0}` with\n   444\t   ds/next. A stream element is either one canonical datom vector or one\n   445\t   atomic `{:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}}` record; transaction\n   446\t   records are flattened into their datoms. :blocked and :end finish the\n   447\t   snapshot at the current tail; :daostream/gap and malformed stream results,\n   448\t   datoms, or transaction records throw. publish-index! calls this to\n   449\t   completion before appending anything to an intake stream.\"\n   450\t  [local-stream]\n   451\t  (loop [cursor {:position 0}\n   452\t         datoms []]\n   453\t    (let [result (ds/next local-stream cursor)]\n   454\t      (cond\n   455\t        (map? result)\n   456\t        (if (and (contains? result :ok) (contains? result :cursor))\n   457\t          (recur (:cursor result)\n   458\t                 (into datoms (stream-payload-datoms (:ok result))))\n   459\t          (throw\n   460\t            (ex-info\n   461\t              \"malformed stream result: a successful read must carry both :ok and :cursor\"\n   462\t              {:result result})))\n   463\t        (= result :blocked) datoms\n   464\t        (= result :end) datoms\n   465\t        (= result :daostream/gap)\n   466\t        (throw (ex-info \"stream snapshot gap: position evicted before read\"\n   467\t                        {:cursor cursor}))\n   468\t        :else (throw (ex-info \"malformed stream signal\" {:signal result}))))))\n   469\t\n   470\t\n   471\t(defn- recording-content-handle\n   472\t  \"Temporary in-memory content store for the publish build. :put-content-fn\n   473\t   records each unique node blob on first insertion (answering :present for\n   474\t   duplicates, so the recorded order is first-insertion order, deduplicated);\n   475\t   :get-content-fn reads recorded blobs back. Addresses are minted by\n   476\t   jing/materialize! through dao.data.btree.storage/kv-storage, so the\n   477\t   recorded order is exactly the store-tree children-before-parent traversal.\n   478\t   The handle is a build-time value: no global state is introduced.\"\n   479\t  []\n   480\t  (let [state (atom {:content {}, :order []})]\n   481\t    {:state state,\n   482\t     :put-content-fn (fn [address payload]\n   483\t                       (if (contains? (:content @state) address)\n   484\t                         :present\n   485\t                         (do (swap! state\n   486\t                                    (fn [s]\n   487\t                                      (-> s\n   488\t                                          (assoc-in [:content address] payload)\n   489\t                                          (update :order conj [address payload]))))\n   490\t                             :inserted))),\n   491\t     :get-content-fn (fn [address not-found]\n   492\t                       (get-in @state [:content address] not-found))}))\n   493\t\n   494\t\n   495\t(defn- validate-branching!\n   496\t  [branching]\n   497\t  (when-not (and (integer? branching) (<= 2 branching))\n   498\t    (throw (ex-info\n   499\t             \"publish-index! :branching-factor must be an integer of at least 2\"\n   500\t             {:branching-factor branching})))\n   501\t  branching)\n   502\t\n   503\t\n   504\t(defn- select-intake-stream!\n   505\t  [intake-pool select-fn]\n   506\t  (when-not (coll? intake-pool)\n   507\t    (throw\n   508\t      (ex-info\n   509\t        \"publish-index! intake-pool must be a collection of writable dao.stream values\"\n   510\t        {:intake-pool intake-pool})))\n   511\t  (when (empty? intake-pool)\n   512\t    (throw (ex-info \"publish-index! intake-pool must be non-empty\"\n   513\t                    {:intake-pool intake-pool})))\n   514\t  (when-not (fn? select-fn)\n   515\t    (throw (ex-info\n   516\t             \"publish-index! :select-stream must be a function of the pool\"\n   517\t             {:select-stream select-fn})))\n   518\t  (let [selected (select-fn intake-pool)]\n   519\t    (when-not (boolean (some #(identical? % selected) intake-pool))\n   520\t      (throw\n   521\t        (ex-info\n   522\t          \"publish-index! :select-stream returned a value outside the intake pool\"\n   523\t          {:selected selected, :intake-pool intake-pool})))\n   524\t    selected))\n   525\t\n   526\t\n   527\t(defn- append-ok!\n   528\t  \"Append one opaque payload to an intake stream; every stream.v2/append!\n   529\t   must answer `:dao.stream/ok`, anything else throws with the result\n   530\t   attached.\"\n   531\t  [stream payload]\n   532\t  (let [result (stream/append! stream payload)]\n   533\t    (when-not (and (map? result) (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome result)))\n   534\t      (throw (ex-info (str \"publish-index! stream append failed: \"\n   535\t                           (pr-str result))\n   536\t                      {:stream stream, :result result, :payload payload})))\n   537\t    result))\n   538\t\n   539\t\n   540\t(defn publish-index!\n   541\t  \"The transactor entry point, agent-side (docs/design/dao.jing.md,\n   542\t   Publication from an agent).\n   543\t\n   544\t   1. Snapshots the agent's local stream (snapshot-datoms) — fully, before\n   545\t      any publication append. Atomic transaction records are flattened here.\n   546\t   2. Builds the four covered indexes into a temporary recording content\n   547\t      handle through dao.data.btree.storage/kv-storage, so addresses are\n   548\t      minted by jing/materialize! and equal blobs deduplicate in\n   549\t      children-before-parent first-insertion order.\n   550\t   3. Appends every unique node blob in that recorded order, then the\n   551\t      manifest, all to exactly the intake stream selected from the pool.\n   552\t   4. Returns {:manifest-address (jing/segment-key manifest) :manifest\n   553\t      manifest}.\n   554\t\n   555\t   The manifest address is derived from the manifest alone and never depends\n   556\t   on which intake stream carried it. A partial immutable prefix on a full\n   557\t   intake stream is acceptable and retry-safe: the manifest is always\n   558\t   appended last, so a :full failure can only have left node blobs.\n   559\t\n   560\t   Success acknowledges that every payload was appended to the selected\n   561\t   intake stream. It does not acknowledge that an asynchronous DaoJing\n   562\t   observer has materialized those payloads yet. Because the build starts at\n   563\t   cursor position zero and reconstructs complete indexes, local-stream must\n   564\t   retain its complete datom history; a retention gap throws before emission.\n   565\t\n   566\t   Usage:\n   567\t     (publish-index! local-stream intake-pool)\n   568\t     (publish-index! local-stream intake-pool\n   569\t                     {:branching-factor n, :select-stream f})\n   570\t\n   571\t   opts: {:branching-factor n — max keys per node, at least 2 (default 512)\n   572\t          :select-stream f   — receives the pool and returns the intake\n   573\t                               stream to append to (default first)}\"\n   574\t  ([local-stream intake-pool] (publish-index! local-stream intake-pool nil))\n   575\t  ([local-stream intake-pool opts]\n   576\t   (let [branching (validate-branching! (:branching-factor opts 512))\n   577\t         intake (select-intake-stream! intake-pool (:select-stream opts first))\n   578\t         datoms (snapshot-datoms local-stream)\n   579\t         handle (recording-content-handle)\n   580\t         storage (bts/kv-storage handle {:branching-factor branching})\n   581\t         build-tree\n   582\t         (fn [cmp]\n   583\t           (bt/from-sequential cmp datoms {:branching-factor branching}))\n   584\t         trees {:eavt (build-tree eavt-cmp),\n   585\t                :aevt (build-tree aevt-cmp),\n   586\t                :avet (build-tree avet-cmp),\n   587\t                :vaet (build-tree vaet-cmp)}\n   588\t         root-addr (fn [tree]\n   589\t                     ;; an empty index has no root node; nil is the\n   590\t                     ;; explicit\n   591\t                     ;; \"nothing here\" (walk of nil => ())\n   592\t                     (when (seq datoms) (bt/store-tree tree storage)))\n   593\t         manifest {:indexes {:eavt (root-addr (:eavt trees)),\n   594\t                             :aevt (root-addr (:aevt trees)),\n   595\t                             :avet (root-addr (:avet trees)),\n   596\t                             :vaet (root-addr (:vaet trees))},\n   597\t                   ;; Covered indexes are sets. Their count must describe\n   598\t                   ;; the distinct tuples actually stored, not duplicate\n   599\t                   ;; occurrences in the source stream.\n   600\t                   :count (bt/count (:eavt trees)),\n   601\t                   :branching-factor branching}]\n   602\t     (doseq [[_ payload] (:order @(:state handle))] (append-ok! intake payload))\n   603\t     (append-ok! intake manifest)\n   604\t     {:manifest-address (jing/segment-key manifest), :manifest manifest})))\n   900\t    (reindex-state {:values values} schema strict?)))\n   901\t\n   902\t\n   903\t(defn- next-transaction-time\n   904\t  [rows]\n   905\t  (if (seq rows)\n   906\t    (inc (reduce max (map index/datom-t rows)))\n   907\t    0))\n   908\t\n   909\t\n   910\t(defn- stamp-candidate\n   911\t  [t d]\n   912\t  (let [[e a v _dt m] d]\n   913\t    [e a v t (if (nil? m) datom/default-op m)]))\n   914\t\n   915\t\n   916\t(declare contains-schema-row?)\n   917\t\n   918\t\n   919\t(defn- proposed-schema\n   920\t  \"Interpret schema rows as the next transaction would, without emitting.\n   921\t   The wrapper and its inner transactor are serialized together, so the\n   922\t   predicted t is the inner transactor's next t.\"\n   923\t  [local-stream schema datoms]\n   924\t  (if-not (contains-schema-row? datoms)\n   925\t    schema\n   926\t    (let [rows (index/snapshot-datoms local-stream)\n   927\t          t (next-transaction-time rows)]\n   928\t      (extract-schema (into rows (map #(stamp-candidate t %) datoms))))))\n   929\t\n   930\t\n   931\t#_{:clj-kondo/ignore [:unused-binding]}\n   932\t\n   933\t\n   934\t(defn- with-write-lock\n   935\t  \"Serialize one wrapper's validate -> append -> state transition. The state\n   936\t   atom is a private per-wrapper coordination handle, never global state.\"\n   937\t  [lock f]\n   938\t  #?(:clj (locking lock (f))\n   939\t     :default (f)))\n   940\t\n   941\t\n   942\t(deftype SchemaWrapper\n   943\t  [inner local-stream strict? state]\n   944\t\n   945\t  ds/IDaoStreamBound\n   946\t\n   947\t  (close!\n   948\t    [_]\n   949\t    (with-write-lock state #(ds/close! inner)))\n   950\t\n   951\t\n   952\t  (closed?\n   953\t    [_]\n   954\t    (ds/closed? inner)))\n   955\t\n   956\t\n   957\t(defn transactor\n   958\t  \"Open a schema-validating wrapper over a local stream and intake pool.\n   959\t   opts: {:strict true} for strict mode (default lax). OWNS the inner\n   960\t   :transactor handle; closing the wrapper delegates inward.\"\n   961\t  ([local-stream intake-pool]\n   962\t   (transactor local-stream intake-pool nil))\n   963\t  ([local-stream intake-pool opts]\n   964\t   (let [strict? (boolean (:strict opts))\n   965\t         inner (ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :transactor\n   966\t                          :local-stream local-stream\n   967\t                          :intake-pool intake-pool\n   968\t                          :name \"schema\"})\n   969\t         rows (index/snapshot-datoms local-stream)\n   970\t         schema (extract-schema rows)]\n   971\t     (->SchemaWrapper inner local-stream strict?\n   972\t                      (atom (rows->state rows schema strict?))))))\n   973\t\n   974\t\n   975\t(defn- contains-schema-row?\n   976\t  \"True when any datom in the batch has an attribute that is one of the\n   977\t   five :db/* property names.\"\n   978\t  [datoms]\n   979\t  (some (fn [d] (schema-row? (second d))) datoms))\n   980\t\n   981\t\n   982\t(defn- validate-record-ops!\n   983\t  \"Reject item-order ambiguity between assertion and retraction for one EAV.\n   984\t   Persisted current-state semantics treats same-t opposite operations as\n   985\t   conflicting history, so such a record cannot cross the append boundary.\"\n   986\t  [datoms]\n   987\t  (reduce\n   988\t    (fn [ops d]\n   989\t      (let [eav (subvec (vec d) 0 3)\n   990\t            op (if (= 5 (count d)) (nth d 4) datom/default-op)]\n   991\t        (when (and (contains? ops eav) (not= (get ops eav) op))\n   992\t          (throw (ex-info (str \"opposite operations on one EAV in a single \"\n   993\t                               \"transaction: \" (pr-str eav))\n   994\t                          {:eav eav, :operations #{(get ops eav) op}})))\n   995\t        (assoc ops eav op)))\n   996\t    {}\n   997\t    datoms)\n   998\t  nil)\n   999\t\n  1000\t\n  1001\t(defn- translated-op\n  1002\t  [d]\n  1003\t  (if (= 5 (count d)) (nth d 4) datom/default-op))\n  1004\t\n  1005\t\n  1006\t(defn- validate-axiom-datoms!\n  1007\t  \"Protect axiom entities using the complete translated record. Looking at\n  1008\t   the whole record makes the rule independent of item order: an axiom ident\n  1009\t   declared in this record protects every row on that entity in this record.\"\n  1010\t  [datoms state]\n  1011\t  (let [established\n  1012\t        (reduce-kv\n  1013\t          (fn [m [e a] values]\n  1014\t            (if (= :db/ident a)\n  1015\t              (reduce (fn [acc ident]\n  1016\t                        (if (contains? axioms ident)\n  1017\t                          (update acc e (fnil conj #{}) ident)\n  1018\t                          acc))\n  1019\t                      m\n  1020\t                      values)\n  1021\t              m))\n  1022\t          {}\n  1023\t          (:values state))\n  1024\t        axiom-idents\n  1025\t        (reduce\n  1026\t          (fn [m d]\n  1027\t            (let [[e a v] d]\n  1028\t              (if (and (= :db/ident a)\n  1029\t                       (= datom/default-op (translated-op d))\n  1030\t                       (contains? axioms v))\n  1031\t                (update m e (fnil conj #{}) v)\n  1032\t                m)))\n  1033\t          established\n  1034\t          datoms)]\n  1035\t    (doseq [[e idents] axiom-idents]\n  1036\t      (when (< 1 (count idents))\n  1037\t        (throw (ex-info (str \"axiom protection: entity \" (pr-str e)\n  1038\t                             \" cannot name multiple axioms \"\n  1039\t                             (pr-str idents))\n  1040\t                        {:entity e, :idents idents}))))\n  1041\t    (doseq [d datoms\n  1042\t            :let [[e a v] d\n  1043\t                  ident (first (get axiom-idents e))]\n  1044\t            :when ident]\n  1045\t      (let [expected (assoc (get axioms ident) :db/ident ident)]\n  1046\t        (when (or (not= datom/default-op (translated-op d))\n  1047\t                  (not (contains? expected a))\n  1048\t                  (not= (get expected a) v))\n  1049\t          (throw (ex-info (str \"axiom protection: \" (pr-str ident)\n  1050\t                               \" permits only its verbatim axiom facts, got \"\n  1051\t                               (pr-str [e a v]))\n  1052\t                          {:entity e, :ident ident, :attr a, :value v,\n  1053\t                           :expected (get expected a),\n  1054\t                           :operation (translated-op d)}))))))\n  1055\t  nil)\n  1056\t\n  1057\t\n  1058\t(defn transact!\n  1059\t  \"Translate, validate, and commit tx-data as one atomic record. Returns\n  1060\t   {:result :ok :t t :datoms datoms}. One per-wrapper serialized transition\n  1061\t   plans and validates the complete next state before the single append, then\n  1062\t   installs that already-planned state only after append succeeds. Schema\n  1063\t   changes become effective for the next transaction.\"\n  1064\t  [^SchemaWrapper wrapper tx-data]\n  1065\t  (let [lock (.-state wrapper)]\n  1066\t    (with-write-lock\n  1067\t      lock\n  1068\t      (fn []\n  1069\t        (when (ds/closed? wrapper)\n  1070\t          (throw (ex-info \"cannot transact! on closed wrapper\" {})))\n  1071\t        (when (empty? tx-data)\n  1072\t          (throw (ex-info \"transact! requires at least one item\"\n  1073\t                          {:tx-data tx-data})))\n  1074\t        (let [st @lock\n  1075\t              schema (:schema st)\n  1076\t              strict? (.-strict? wrapper)\n  1077\t              translated\n  1078\t              (loop [items (vec tx-data)\n  1079\t                     datoms []\n  1080\t                     state st]\n  1081\t                (if (empty? items)\n  1082\t                  {:datoms datoms, :state state}\n  1083\t                  (let [item (first items)\n  1084\t                        result (translate-record item state schema strict?)]\n  1085\t                    ;; Both-mode schema structure validation is part of the\n  1086\t                    ;; plan, before any append can occur.\n  1087\t                    (doseq [d (:datoms result)]\n  1088\t                      (validate-schema-row! (nth d 0) (nth d 1) (nth d 2)\n  1089\t                                            (:state result)))\n  1090\t                    (recur (rest items)\n  1091\t                           (into datoms (:datoms result))\n  1092\t                           (:state result)))))\n  1093\t              datoms (:datoms translated)]\n  1094\t          (when (empty? datoms)\n  1095\t            (throw (ex-info \"transact! produced no datoms\"\n  1096\t                            {:tx-data tx-data})))\n  1097\t          (validate-record-ops! datoms)\n  1098\t          (validate-axiom-datoms! datoms st)\n  1099\t          (let [schema-change? (boolean (contains-schema-row? datoms))\n  1100\t                next-schema (proposed-schema (.-local-stream wrapper)\n  1101\t                                             schema\n  1102\t                                             datoms)\n  1103\t                _ (validate-unique-card-one! next-schema)\n  1104\t                next-state (if schema-change?\n  1105\t                             (reindex-state (:state translated)\n  1106\t                                            next-schema\n  1107\t                                            strict?)\n  1108\t                             (-> (:state translated)\n  1109\t                                 (dissoc :datoms)\n  1110\t                                 (assoc :schema next-schema\n  1111\t                                        :strict? strict?)))\n  1112\t                result (tx/transact! (.-inner wrapper) datoms)]\n  1113\t            (reset! lock next-state)\n  1114\t            result))))))\n  1115\t\n  1116\t\n  1117\t;; =============================================================================\n  1118\t;; Publisher: publish! and published descriptor (§5)\n  1119\t;; =============================================================================\n  1120\t\n  1121\t(defn publish!\n  1122\t  \"Build and enqueue covered indexes over the wrapper's local stream.\n  1123\t   Thin passthrough to the inner handle's dao.space.transactor/publish!.\n  1124\t   Returns {:manifest-address ... :manifest ...}.\"\n  1125\t  ([^SchemaWrapper wrapper] (publish! wrapper nil))\n  1126\t  ([^SchemaWrapper wrapper opts]\n  1127\t   (when (ds/closed? wrapper)\n  1128\t     (throw (ex-info \"cannot publish! on closed wrapper\" {})))\n  1129\t   (tx/publish! (.-inner wrapper) opts)))\n  1130\t\n  1131\t\n  1132\t(defn published\n  1133\t  \"Construct a §5 published descriptor for one immutable covered-index\n  1134\t   manifest. content-store is a serializable DaoJing coordinate (map with\n  1135\t   :dao.jing/type); manifest-address is a segment content address.\n  1136\t   Validates like index/published-index but carries the schema type.\"\n  1137\t  [content-store manifest-address]\n  1138\t  (when-not (and (map? content-store) (keyword? (:dao.jing/type content-store)))\n  1139\t    (throw (ex-info \"published requires a DaoJing store coordinate\"\n  1140\t                    {:content-store content-store})))\n  1141\t  (when-not (jing/segment-address? manifest-address)\n  1142\t    (throw (ex-info \"published requires a manifest content address\"\n  1143\t                    {:manifest-address manifest-address})))\n  1144\t  {:dao.stream/type :dao.space.schema/published\n  1145\t   :dao.stream/bound {:manifest-address manifest-address}\n  1146\t   :dao.stream/comparator :dao.space.index/eavt\n  1147\t   :content-store content-store\n  1148\t   :manifest-address manifest-address})\n  1149\t\n  1150\t\n  1151\t#_{:clj-kondo/ignore [:unresolved-symbol :unresolved-var :private-call]}\n  1152\t\n  1153\t\n  1154\t(ds/defopen :dao.space.schema/published\n  1155\t            [descriptor]\n  1156\t            (let [{:keys [content-store manifest-address]} descriptor\n  1157\t                  expected (published content-store manifest-address)]\n  1158\t              (when-not (= expected descriptor)\n  1159\t                (throw (ex-info \"invalid schema/published descriptor\"\n  1160\t                                {:descriptor descriptor, :expected expected})))\n  1161\t              (let [r (ds/open! (index/published-index content-store manifest-address))]\n  1162\t                ;; The btree is lazily loaded from the store.  Eagerly force all\n  1163\t                ;; datoms now so the data is cached in memory.  When schema/current\n  1164\t                ;; later calls ds/close! (which closes the jing store) and then\n  1165\t                ;; interpret-view, the datoms delay is already realized and next()\n  1166\t                ;; reads from the cached vector — no store access needed.\n  1167\t                (ds/strict-vec r)\n  1168\t                r)))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"defn read-datoms|defn read-manifest|defn open-published|defn published-index|defrecord PublishedIndex|defn snapshot-datoms|stream-payload-datoms\\\" src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc | sed -n '250,475p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc | sed -n '220,355p'; rg -n \\\"schema/published|published\\\" test/dao/space/schema_test.cljc | tail -80; rg -n \\\"open-published-rejects|published-open|published-next|transportable|covered-indexes|empty-index|failed open|closes\\\" test/dao/space/index_test.cljc test/dao/space/query_test.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
